| `void main()` | `main` must return `int` in standard C. | Change to `int main(void)` (or `int main(int argc, char *argv[])`). |
| `int *p = a` | The pointer points to the first element of `a`. | This is fine; it’s the intended use. |
| Scanning with `p++` | After the first loop, `p` points **past the last element** (`a+N`). | Reset `p` to the beginning of the array (`p = a;`) before the second loop. |
| Printing with `p++` | You’re dereferencing memory that is **outside** the array (`a+N` onwards). This is undefined behavior and will usually crash. | Reset `p` (or use an index) before printing. |
| Not checking `scanf` return value | If the user enters a non‑integer, the program will read garbage. | Check the return value of `scanf`. |
| No return statement from `main` | The function should return an exit status. | Return `0;` at the end of `main`. |

### Fixed version

```c
#include <stdio.h>

#define N 5

int main(void)
{
    int a[N];
    int *p;
    int i;

    /* Read values */
    p = a;                    /* point to the first element */
    for (i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        if (scanf("%d", p) != 1) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Error reading integer %d\n", i + 1);
            return 1;
        }
        ++p;                  /* advance to next element */
    }

    /* Print values */
    p = a;                    /* reset to the start of the array */
    for (i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        printf("%d ", *p++);
    }
    printf("\n");

    return 0;
}
```

#### Key take‑aways

1. **Always reset pointers** before re‑using them for a new pass over the data.  
2. `void main()` is non‑standard; use `int main(void)` instead.  
3. Resetting `p` to the base of the array before the second loop keeps all accesses inside bounds and prevents undefined behavior.  
4. Checking the return value of `scanf` is good defensive programming.

With these corrections the program will read five integers into `a` and then print them out correctly.

