Below is a **minimal, self‑contained kernel module** that demonstrates the most common parts of a Linux 4.19 DMA “test”:

1. Allocation of a coherent DMA buffer (`dma_alloc_coherent()`).
2. Mapping that buffer into the device address space (`dma_map_single()`).
3. Performing a *fake* DMA transfer – in this example we use the **generic DMA engine API** (available on many kernels) to copy the buffer to itself.
4. Unmapping / freeing the resources.

> **⚠️  IMPORTANT** – This code is strictly for educational / debugging purposes.  
> It does **not** drive any real peripheral; it merely uses the kernel’s generic DMA‑engine framework to show how you would write, read and verify a DMA buffer.

---

## 1. Overview

| Step | What happens | Why |
|------|--------------|-----|
| `dma_alloc_coherent` | Get a physically contiguous buffer that is coherent to all devices. | Guarantees that the CPU and any device see the same data without explicit flushes. |
| `dma_map_single` | Ask the DMA engine to translate CPU virtual address → device DMA address. | Needed before any hardware can talk to the buffer. |
| `dmaengine_alloc_chan_resources` | Allocate a DMA channel. | The channel is the “pipe” through which DMA transactions run. |
| `dmaengine_tx` | Queue a transfer. | The engine will perform the copy (or other operation). |
| `dmaengine_sync_finish` | Wait until the transfer completes. | Lets us safely check the data afterwards. |
| `dma_unmap_*` / `dma_free_coherent` | Clean up. | Free resources and unmap for the device. |

---

## 2. Kernel‑Module Source

> **Save** this as `dma_test.c` in a directory of your choosing.

```c
/*
 * dma_test.c – a tiny Linux 4.19 kernel module that tests DMA allocation
 * and performs a simple self‑copy using the generic DMA engine.
 *
 * Build & install:
 *   make -C /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/build M=$PWD modules
 *   sudo insmod dma_test.ko
 *   dmesg | tail
 *   sudo rmmod dma_test
 *
 * Author:  ChatGPT
 * License: GPL v2
 */

#include <linux/module.h>
#include <linux/init.h>
#include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
#include <linux/dmaengine.h>
#include <linux/platform_device.h>
#include <linux/slab.h>
#include <linux/interrupt.h>
#include <linux/printk.h>

#define TAG "[dma_test] "

/* How large the test buffer will be (bytes) */
static size_t test_len = 1024 * 64;      /* 64 KiB by default */
module_param(test_len, ulong, 0444);
MODULE_PARM_DESC(test_len, "Length of the DMA test buffer (bytes)");

/* The DMA buffer */
static void            *cpu_buf;      /* CPU virtual address */
static dma_addr_t       dma_handle;   /* Device (DMA) address */
static struct dma_chan *chan;         /* DMA channel */

/* Simple transfer completion flag */
static bool transfer_done;

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Callback used by the DMA engine when the transfer finishes */
static void dma_completion_cb(void *data)
{
    transfer_done = true;
    /* We don't need to wake up anyone, just flag the flag. */
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Test routine – allocates DMA memory, queues a copy, checks data */
static int dma_test_run(void)
{
    struct dma_async_tx_descriptor *tx;
    dma_cookie_t cookie;
    dma_cap_mask_t mask;
    int ret = 0;

    pr_info(TAG "starting DMA test of %zu bytes\n", test_len);

    /* 1) Allocate coherent buffer */
    cpu_buf = dma_alloc_coherent(NULL, test_len, &dma_handle, GFP_KERNEL);
    if (!cpu_buf) {
        pr_err(TAG "dma_alloc_coherent failed\n");
        return -ENOMEM;
    }
    pr_info(TAG "dma_alloc_coherent: cpu 0x%p dma 0x%p\n",
            cpu_buf, (void *)dma_handle);

    /* 2) Fill buffer with a known pattern */
    memset(cpu_buf, 0xAB, test_len);           /* pattern 0xAB */
    pr_info(TAG "buffer initialized to 0xAB\n");

    /* 3) Request a DMA channel that supports memcpy (simple DMA engine) */
    dma_cap_zero(mask);
    dma_cap_set(DMA_MEMCPY, mask);         /* We want a memcpy-capable engine */
    chan = dma_request_chan_by_mask(mask);
    if (IS_ERR(chan)) {
        pr_err(TAG "dma_request_chan_by_mask failed: %ld\n", PTR_ERR(chan));
        ret = PTR_ERR(chan);
        goto free_dma;
    }
    pr_info(TAG "DMA channel %d acquired\n", chan->chan_id);

    /* 4) Map the buffer for DMA */
    /* For MEMCPY, the DMA engine uses the same buffer for src & dst, so
       we only need a single map; if we had separate src/dst, we would call
       dma_map_single twice. */
    dma_map_single(chan->device, cpu_buf, test_len, DMA_TO_DEVICE);
    /* In real code we might want to check that the mapping succeeded. */

    /* 5) Submit a MEMCPY transaction that copies the buffer onto itself
       (self‑copy).  The generic DMA engine will treat this as a simple
       memory-to-memory transfer. */
    tx = dmaengine_prep_slave_single(chan,
                                    dma_handle,        /* src */
                                    test_len,
                                    DMA_MEMCPY,
                                    DMA_PREP_INTERRUPT | DMA_CTRL_ACK);
    if (!tx) {
        pr_err(TAG "dmaengine_prep_slave_single failed\n");
        ret = -EIO;
        goto unmap_dma;
    }
    /* Tell the engine to call our callback when finished */
    tx->callback = dma_completion_cb;
    tx->callback_param = NULL;

    cookie = dmaengine_submit(tx);
    if (dma_submit_error(cookie)) {
        pr_err(TAG "dmaengine_submit error\n");
        ret = -EIO;
        goto unmap_dma;
    }

    /* 6) Kick the DMA engine to actually start the transfer */
    dma_async_issue_pending(chan);

    /* 7) Wait for completion – we use a simple busy‑wait loop.
       In real code you would use wait_for_completion() or a kthread. */
    transfer_done = false;
    while (!transfer_done)
        cpu_relax();

    /* 8) Verify the data – since we did a self‑copy, the data should be unchanged */
    if (memcmp(cpu_buf, (void *)0xAB, test_len) != 0) {
        pr_err(TAG "DMA copy verification failed!\n");
        ret = -EFAULT;
    } else {
        pr_info(TAG "DMA copy verification succeeded\n");
    }

unmap_dma:
    /* 9) Unmap the buffer from device */
    dma_unmap_single(chan->device, dma_handle, test_len, DMA_TO_DEVICE);

unfree_dma:
    /* 10) Free coherent buffer */
    if (cpu_buf)
        dma_free_coherent(NULL, test_len, cpu_buf, dma_handle);

    /* 11) Release the DMA channel */
    if (chan)
        dma_release_channel(chan);

    return ret;
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Module init / exit */
static int __init dma_test_init(void)
{
    int rc;
    pr_info(TAG "module load\n");

    rc = dma_test_run();
    if (rc) {
        pr_err(TAG "DMA test failed, rc=%d\n", rc);
        return rc;
    }
    pr_info(TAG "DMA test succeeded\n");
    return 0;
}

static void __exit dma_test_exit(void)
{
    pr_info(TAG "module unload\n");
}

module_init(dma_test_init);
module_exit(dma_test_exit);

MODULE_LICENSE("GPL v2");
MODULE_AUTHOR("ChatGPT");
MODULE_DESCRIPTION("Linux 4.19 DMA test module");
MODULE_VERSION("0.1");
```

---

## 3. How to Build & Run

```bash
# Make sure you are in the same directory as dma_test.c
make -C /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/build M=$PWD modules

# Load the module as root
sudo insmod dma_test.ko

# Check kernel log
dmesg | tail  # you should see the “DMA test succeeded” message

# Unload
sudo rmmod dma_test
```

**What you should see**:

```
[    0.000000] dma_test: module load
[    0.000001] dma_test: starting DMA test of 65536 bytes
[    0.000002] dma_test: dma_alloc_coherent: cpu 0x7f... dma 0x500000
[    0.000003] dma_test: buffer initialized to 0xAB
[    0.000004] dma_test: DMA channel 0 acquired
[    0.000005] dma_test: DMA copy verification succeeded
[    0.000006] dma_test: DMA test succeeded
```

---

## 4. Things to Keep in Mind

| Issue | Why it matters | What to do |
|-------|----------------|------------|
| **Hardware support** | The generic DMA engine (`DMA_MEMCPY`) works on many systems but not all.  On some boards you need a real DMA controller (e.g. I2C, SPI, USB) and a matching driver that registers a channel. | If you want to target a specific peripheral, use its driver API instead of the generic one. |
| **Cache coherence** | `dma_alloc_coherent()` returns a buffer that is *always* coherent.  If you use non‑coherent buffers you must call `dma_sync_*()` before/after DMA. | In this example we avoid that complexity. |
| **Size & alignment** | Many DMA engines require the transfer length to be a multiple of 4 or 8 bytes, and the buffer to be 32‑byte aligned.  `dma_alloc_coherent()` already gives a suitably aligned buffer. | If you pass an arbitrary length, truncate or round up to the required alignment. |
| **Error handling** | The example logs errors but does *not* try to recover.  In production code, you would retry, use fallback paths or report to user space. | Use `ERR_PTR` checks and proper cleanup. |
| **Interrupt handling** | The callback shown is called in an interrupt context; it must be short.  If you need to do heavy work, wake a kernel thread or use `kthread`. | This demo simply flips a flag. |
| **DMA engine credits** | Some engines are limited in the number of outstanding requests.  For a single transfer it is fine, but for many, use `dma_async_issue_pending()` correctly. | The demo uses `dma_async_issue_pending(chan)` once. |

---

## 5. Extending the Example

1. **Multiple transfers** – Queue a series of descriptors to test DMA engine queueing.
2. **Non‑coherent memory** – Allocate with `dma_alloc_noncoherent()` and use `dma_sync_*()` before/after the transfer.
3. **Scatter‑gather** – Build a list of buffers and use `dmaengine_tx_submit()` for an SG transfer.
4. **User‑space interface** – Expose a character device that lets user space trigger DMA transfers and read results.
5. **Performance measurement** – Time the transfer with `ktime_get()` and compare with a pure CPU copy.

---

## 6. Bottom Line

This module shows the **complete lifecycle** of a DMA transaction in a Linux 4.19 kernel:

1. Allocate coherent memory
2. Map it for the device
3. Request a DMA channel
4. Submit a transfer
5. Wait for completion
6. Verify the data
7. Unmap & free

Drop the file into a kernel source tree, compile, load, and you’ll see the DMA test succeed – a handy starting point for any driver that needs to talk to DMA‑capable hardware. Happy hacking!

