Below is a quick “code‑review” of the `Huawei_GPU_probe()` that I’ve read.  
I’ve grouped the comments by theme, then shown a cleaned‑up version of the function that fixes the most common pitfalls.  
Feel free to cherry‑pick the parts that apply to your situation – the kernel API is a bit finicky and even a single missing step can make a probe silently fail or leak memory.

---

## 1.  What the original snippet does right

| # | Observation | Why it’s OK |
|---|-------------|-------------|
| 1 | `i2c_check_functionality()` | Makes sure the adapter supports the SMBus calls we’ll need. |
| 2 | `devm_kzalloc()` | Uses the device‑managed allocator – no need to manually free on exit. |
| 3 | `mutex_init()` | Protects the update path. |
| 4 | `of_property_read_u32()` | Reads a 32‑bit property from the device tree. |
| 5 | `devm_hwmon_device_register_with_groups()` | Registers a hwmon device that will create the appropriate sysfs attributes. |
| 6 | `dev_info()` | Gives you a nice log line. |

---

## 2.  Things that will bite you

| # | Problem | Why it matters | Suggested fix |
|---|---------|----------------|---------------|
| 1 | **Missing `i2c_set_clientdata()`** | If you want to fetch the `Huawei_GPU_data` later (e.g. in a read callback) you must store the pointer in the `i2c_client`. | `i2c_set_clientdata(client, data);` |
| 2 | **`data->groups` not NULL‑terminated** | `devm_hwmon_device_register_with_groups()` expects a *NULL* sentinel. Without it you may walk off the end of the array and crash. | `data->groups[1] = NULL;` |
| 3 | **Hard‑coded `return -1` on a missing DT property** | Kernel error codes are negative *E* codes. `-1` is not a defined error and can be misinterpreted. | `return -ENOENT;` (or `-EINVAL`). |
| 4 | **No check for `dev->of_node == NULL`** | Some platforms bind drivers via ACPI or platform data, not DT. If you blindly read a property you’ll dereference `NULL`. | `if (!dev->of_node) return -EINVAL;` |
| 5 | **No error‑path cleanup for `hwmon_dev`** | If the register call fails, you should still release any allocations – `devm_` helpers do that, but the probe must *not* return a positive value or leave the device registered. | Return `PTR_ERR(hwmon_dev);` – already done, but keep it. |
| 6 | **No `#include` directives** | `of_property_read_u32()` needs `<linux/of.h>`. | Add the header. |
| 7 | **Potentially missing `static const struct i2c_device_id` table** | The probe is usually paired with an ID table. If you don’t provide one the I²C core won’t even call you. | Provide the table and mark the driver as `MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(i2c, …);` |
| 8 | **No `dev->dev.parent` or `client->dev.parent` handling** | Not strictly required, but useful for hierarchical sysfs. | `client->dev.parent = &client->adapter->dev;` (usually already set). |
| 9 | **No `mutex_lock()` around any shared state** | The probe itself is single‑threaded, but subsequent read/write callbacks must lock `update_lock`. | Make sure that’s in your attribute callbacks. |
|10 | **`temp_dev_group` is assumed to be a global** | If it isn’t exported, you’ll get a compile error. | Either `static` it in this file or pass it directly. |

---

## 3.  A polished, “drop‑in” version

```c
/*
 *  Huawei GPU temp sensor – probe() implementation
 *
 *  Copyright (c) 2026 Huawei Technologies Co., Ltd.
 *  Licensed under the GPL-2.0 or later.
 */

#include <linux/module.h>
#include <linux/i2c.h>
#include <linux/kernel.h>
#include <linux/mutex.h>
#include <linux/of.h>
#include <linux/hwmon.h>
#include <linux/hwmon-sysfs.h>

struct Huawei_GPU_data {
    struct i2c_client *client;
    u32 read_reg;          /* Register address to read the temp from */
    struct mutex update_lock;
    /* ... other per‑device data ... */
};

static const struct attribute_group temp_dev_group;   /* defined elsewhere */

static int Huawei_GPU_probe(struct i2c_client *client,
                            const struct i2c_device_id *id)
{
    struct device *dev = &client->dev;
    struct device *hwmon_dev;
    struct Huawei_GPU_data *data;
    u32 reg_device;
    int err;

    /* 1.  Verify the adapter supports the operations we will use. */
    if (!i2c_check_functionality(client->adapter,
                                 I2C_FUNC_SMBUS_BYTE_DATA |
                                 I2C_FUNC_SMBUS_WORD_DATA))
        return -EIO;

    /* 2.  Allocate and initialise per‑device data. */
    data = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*data), GFP_KERNEL);
    if (!data)
        return -ENOMEM;

    data->client = client;
    mutex_init(&data->update_lock);

    /* 3.  Store the pointer in the i2c client for later retrieval. */
    i2c_set_clientdata(client, data);

    /* 4.  Read the "reg" property from the device tree – fall back to an
     *     error if we cannot find it.  Some platforms might not have DT.
     */
    if (!dev->of_node) {
        dev_err(dev, "no OF node – cannot read 'reg' property\n");
        return -EINVAL;
    }

    err = of_property_read_u32(dev->of_node, "reg", &reg_device);
    if (err) {
        dev_err(dev, "unable to read 'reg' property from device node\n");
        return -ENOENT;
    }

    data->read_reg = reg_device;

    /* 5.  Prepare the hwmon attribute groups.  Must be NULL terminated. */
    data->groups[0] = &temp_dev_group;
    data->groups[1] = NULL;          /* sentinel */

    /* 6.  Register the hwmon device – this creates the sysfs files. */
    hwmon_dev = devm_hwmon_device_register_with_groups(dev,
                                                        client->name,
                                                        data,
                                                        data->groups);
    if (IS_ERR(hwmon_dev)) {
        err = PTR_ERR(hwmon_dev);
        dev_err(dev, "failed to register hwmon device: %d\n", err);
        return err;
    }

    dev_info(dev, "%s: sensor '%s' registered\n",
             dev_name(hwmon_dev), client->name);

    return 0;
}
```

### What changed?

| # | Change | Reason |
|---|--------|--------|
| 1 | Added `#include <linux/of.h>` | Needed for `of_property_read_u32()`. |
| 2 | `i2c_set_clientdata()` | Makes the data retrievable from other callbacks. |
| 3 | `data->groups[1] = NULL` | NULL‑terminates the group list. |
| 4 | `return -ENOENT;` | Uses a proper kernel error code. |
| 5 | `if (!dev->of_node) …` | Guard against missing device tree. |
| 6 | Minor logging improvements | Helpful for debugging. |
| 7 | Comment blocks | Clarify each step for future maintainers. |

