0eeb7414b228dea2e49788e6382151f5.RESPONSE:
**Solution Overview**

The classic “missing‑square tromino” problem is a textbook divide‑and‑conquer exercise.  
For a board of size `2^n × 2^n` with one square removed we can always tile the
rest of the board with L‑shaped trominoes.  
The algorithm works as follows:

1. **Base case** – a `2 × 2` board  
   The three remaining squares form a single tromino.  
   According to the position of the missing square the tromino is printed
   with one of four labels

   | missing square | tromino label |
   |----------------|---------------|
   | upper‑left     | `"LR"`        |
   | upper‑right    | `"LL"`        |
   | lower‑right    | `"UL"`        |
   | lower‑left     | `"UR"`        |

   The missing square is printed as `"MS"`.

2. **Recursive step** – board size `> 2`  
   Split the board into four `half × half` sub‑boards.  
   Place one central tromino that covers the four *central* squares of the
   sub‑boards – the square that lies in the same quadrant as the real missing
   square is left uncovered (it becomes the “missing square” for the other
   three sub‑boards).  
   The orientation of the central tromino is chosen exactly as in the base
   case, because the four central squares form a virtual `2 × 2` board.

   After that we recurse on each of the four sub‑boards, giving the missing
   square of each sub‑board either the original missing square or the centre
   square that we just occupied.

The algorithm runs in **O(N²)** time, where `N = 2^n` is the side length
of the board, because each square is written to exactly once.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Data structures

```cpp
vector<vector<string>> board;     // board[y][x], origin (0,0) = lower left
```

The coordinate system used in the problem statement (`(0,0)` is the lower
left corner) is mapped to array indices exactly the same way.  
When we print the board we iterate from the top row (`y = boardSize-1`) down
to the bottom row (`y = 0`).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Helper: orientation for a 2×2 board

```cpp
const string orient[2][2] = { {"UR","UL"},
                              {"LR","LL"} };
// orient[dy][dx]   dy = 0 → lower row, 1 → upper row
//                    dx = 0 → left column, 1 → right column
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Function `void tromino(...)`

```cpp
void tromino(int x_board, int y_board,
             int x_missing, int y_missing,
             int board_size)
{
    if (board_size == 2) {                 // ---------- base case ----------
        // coordinates of the 4 cells in the 2×2 square
        int xs[2] = {x_board, x_board + 1};
        int ys[2] = {y_board, y_board + 1};

        // write the missing square
        board[y_missing][x_missing] = "MS";

        // write the three tromino squares
        for (int i = 0; i < 2; ++i)
            for (int j = 0; j < 2; ++j)
                if (!(xs[i]==x_missing && ys[j]==y_missing))
                    board[ys[j]][xs[i]] = orient[ys[j]-y_board][xs[i]-x_board];
        return;
    }

    // ---------- recursive case ----------
    int half = board_size / 2;

    // determine in which quadrant the missing square lies
    bool inLower = y_missing < y_board + half;
    bool inLeft  = x_missing < x_board + half;

    // coordinates of the four centre squares
    int cx = x_board + half - 1;
    int cy = y_board + half - 1;

    // 1. place the central tromino
    //   – the square that belongs to the quadrant with the real missing
    //     square is left empty
    for (int dx = 0; dx < 2; ++dx)
        for (int dy = 0; dy < 2; ++dy) {
            int cx2 = cx + dx;
            int cy2 = cy + dy;
            if ( (inLeft  && dx==0) || (inLeft  && dx==1 && inLower)
                 || (inLeft  && dx==0 && !inLower)
                 || (inLeft  && dx==1 && !inLower) ) {
                // this is the centre square that lies in the same quadrant
                // as the real missing square – skip it
            }
            else
                board[cy2][cx2] = orient[dy][dx];
        }

    // 2. recursive calls for the four sub‑boards
    //    For each sub‑board, the missing square is either
    //    the original missing square or the centre square we just occupied.
    //    The centre square coordinates are:
    //      lower‑left  : (cx,   cy)
    //      lower‑right : (cx+1, cy)
    //      upper‑left  : (cx,   cy+1)
    //      upper‑right : (cx+1, cy+1)

    // lower‑left
    tromino(x_board, y_board,
            inLeft && inLower ? x_missing : cx,
            inLeft && inLower ? y_missing : cy,
            half);

    // lower‑right
    tromino(x_board + half, y_board,
            inLeft && !inLower ? x_missing : cx + 1,
            inLeft && !inLower ? y_missing : cy,
            half);

    // upper‑left
    tromino(x_board, y_board + half,
            !inLeft && inLower ? x_missing : cx,
            !inLeft && inLower ? y_missing : cy + 1,
            half);

    // upper‑right
    tromino(x_board + half, y_board + half,
            !inLeft && !inLower ? x_missing : cx + 1,
            !inLeft && !inLower ? y_missing : cy + 1,
            half);
}
