**What was changed**

1. **Keep track of the previous element** – a new array `prev` stores, for every `arr[i]`, the index of the element that precedes it in the longest consecutive‑(+1) subsequence that ends at `i`.
2. **Tell the caller where the longest subsequence ends** – the function now accepts a pointer `end_idx` that is filled with the index of the last element of the longest subsequence.
3. **Reconstruct the indices** – after the call to `function` we walk backward from `end_idx` through the `prev` array to collect the indices, then print them.
4. Minor clean‑ups (removed unused `freq` in `main`, added `malloc` for `prev`, and included `<stdlib.h>`).

---

```c
/*  longest consecutive subsequence (each element = previous + 1)  */
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

/*  arr   – the input array
    n     – number of elements
    prev  – array of size n; after the call prev[i] holds the index
            of the element that precedes arr[i] in the subsequence
            (or -1 if arr[i] starts a subsequence)
    end_idx – pointer to an int that will receive the index of the
              last element of the longest subsequence
    return – length of the longest subsequence
*/
int function(int arr[], int n, int *prev, int *end_idx)
{
    if (n == 0) {                     /* empty input */
        *end_idx = -1;
        return 0;
    }

    int *freq = malloc(n * sizeof(int));   /* length of subsequence ending at i */
    int max_len = 1;
    int max_index = 0;                    /* index of the last element of longest subseq */

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        freq[i] = 1;          /* subsequence containing only arr[i] */
        prev[i] = -1;         /* nothing comes before it yet */

        for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
            if (arr[j] + 1 == arr[i] && freq[j] + 1 > freq[i]) {
                freq[i] = freq[j] + 1;
                prev[i] = j;          /* remember predecessor */

                if (freq[i] > max_len) {
                    max_len   = freq[i];
                    max_index = i;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    free(freq);
    *end_idx = max_index;      /* give caller the ending position */
    return max_len;
}

int main(void)
{
    int n;
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n <= 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Invalid array size.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    int *arr  = malloc(n * sizeof(int));
    int *prev = malloc(n * sizeof(int));   /* will be filled by function */

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (scanf("%d", &arr[i]) != 1) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Failed to read element %d.\n", i);
            free(arr); free(prev);
            return 1;
        }
    }

    int end_idx;
    int length = function(arr, n, prev, &end_idx);

    printf("%d\n", length);           /* length of longest subsequence */

    if (length > 0) {
        /* reconstruct indices from back to front */
        int *indices = malloc(length * sizeof(int));
        int pos = length - 1;
        int cur = end_idx;

        while (cur != -1) {
            indices[pos--] = cur;    /* store index */
            cur = prev[cur];
        }

        printf("Indices of longest subsequence (0‑based): ");
        for (int i = 0; i < length; i++)
            printf("%d ", indices[i]);
        printf("\n");

        free(indices);
    }

    free(arr);
    free(prev);
    return 0;
}
```

**How it works**

* `function` keeps a `freq` array that, just like before, holds the length of the longest consecutive subsequence ending at each position.
* For every time we extend a subsequence (`arr[j] + 1 == arr[i]`), we store `j` in `prev[i]`.  
  This creates a linked‑list trail that can be followed backward to rebuild the whole subsequence.
* The caller (`main`) receives the index of the last element (`end_idx`) and the `prev` array, then walks from that end backward, collecting all indices, and finally prints them.

Feel free to change the index printing format (e.g., 1‑based indices) if that is what you prefer.

