| `average_no_arg()` | **None** | `void` (prints the average) | Uses a **global** array (so you don’t have to pass anything) and prints the result. |
| `average(int *a, size_t n)` | A pointer to the data and its length | `double` | Computes and returns the average.  The caller decides what to do with it. |

Feel free to drop the code into a single C file and compile it with `gcc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -O2 avg.c -o avg`.

---

### 1. No‑argument, no‑return version

```c
/* avg_no_arg.c */
#include <stdio.h>

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
   Global data – the function does NOT take any parameters.  
   (You can change the values or size as you like – but the
    function itself knows nothing about the caller's context.)
   ------------------------------------------------------------------*/
static double data[] = { 1.0, 3.5, 8.2, 4.4, 9.9 };
static const size_t data_len = sizeof(data) / sizeof(data[0]);

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
   average_no_arg
   ------------------------------------------------------------------*/
void average_no_arg(void)
{
    if (data_len == 0) {
        printf("No data – cannot compute an average.\n");
        return;
    }

    double sum = 0.0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < data_len; ++i)
        sum += data[i];

    double avg = sum / data_len;
    printf("Average (no‑arg version) = %.3f\n", avg);
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
   main – just a demo of the no‑arg version.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------*/
int main(void)
{
    average_no_arg();          /* prints the average */
    return 0;
}
```

> **Why it has no parameters?**  
> Because the function pulls its data from the global `data` array.  
> **Why no return value?**  
> Because the function is asked to “do the work” – it prints the result instead of giving it back.

---

### 2. Argument‑passing, returning average

```c
/* avg_with_args.c */
#include <stdio.h>

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
   average
   ------------------------------------------------------------------*/
double average(const double *a, size_t n)
{
    if (n == 0) {
        /* Returning NaN (not a number) is a common “error” convention. */
        return 0.0 / 0.0;            /* NaN */
    }

    double sum = 0.0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i)
        sum += a[i];

    return sum / n;                  /* caller receives the average */
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
   main – shows how the caller supplies data and receives the result.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------*/
int main(void)
{
    double my_vals[] = { 1.0, 3.5, 8.2, 4.4, 9.9 };
    size_t len = sizeof(my_vals) / sizeof(my_vals[0]);

    double avg = average(my_vals, len);
    if (avg != avg) { /* NaN test – because NaN != NaN */
        printf("Cannot compute average – empty array.\n");
    } else {
        printf("Average (args version) = %.3f\n", avg);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

> **Why take parameters?**  
> Because the caller supplies the data and its length – the function is *pure* and doesn’t rely on any external state.  
> **Why return?**  
> The function hands the computed average back so the caller can use it in further calculations, store it, or print it later.

---

