**Solution Explanation**

We read the input token by token (a token is a maximal sequence of non‑whitespace
characters).  
The sequence ends when the token `"."` is found.  
Every other token must represent an integer – we check this with `strtol`.  
If a token is not a valid integer we simply skip it – that is the “safety from
incorrect input” required in the statement.

All numbers are stored in a singly linked list.  
After the list is built

* if it is empty – we print `NULL` and finish;
* otherwise we swap the *nodes*:

```
first ↔ last
second ↔ second‑last
...
```

The requirement is to exchange the nodes themselves, **not only their
information**.  
Because a singly linked list cannot be traversed backwards, we copy all node
pointers into an array, perform the swaps in that array and then rebuild the
links according to the new order.

Finally we print the data of the list in order.

The program uses only O(n) memory (the list + the pointer array) and
O(n) time – easily inside the limits.



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Algorithm
```
read tokens until "." is read
    if token is a valid integer
        create a new list node with that integer
        append it to the end of the list
    else
        ignore the token   // safety from bad input

if the list is empty
    print "NULL" and exit

// --- swap nodes ----------------------------------------------------
collect all node pointers into an array arr[0 … n-1]
for i = 0 … (n/2)-1
        swap(arr[i], arr[n-1-i])       // node swapping

// rebuild the list according to the new order
for i = 0 … n-2
        arr[i]->next = arr[i+1]
arr[n-1]->next = NULL
head = arr[0]

// --- output ---------------------------------------------------------
traverse the list from head, printing each data value separated by a space
print a newline
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Correctness Proof  

We prove that the algorithm prints the list after swapping first with last,
second with second‑last, and so on.

---

##### Lemma 1  
After building the list, its nodes are in the exact order of the numbers
that were read from the input (ignoring invalid tokens).

**Proof.**  
The algorithm appends each new node to the `tail`.  
Initially the list is empty.  
For the *k*-th valid number we create a node and set `tail->next = node`,
then `tail = node`.  
Thus node *k* becomes the new last node, and the list order is
exactly the input order. ∎



##### Lemma 2  
After collecting node pointers into array `arr`,  
`arr[i]` points to the *i*-th node of the original list
(0‑based indexing).

**Proof.**  
During collection we traverse the list from the head,
storing each visited node in consecutive array positions.
No node is skipped or visited twice.
Hence the *i*-th visited node (which is the *i*-th node in the list)
is stored in `arr[i]`. ∎



##### Lemma 3  
After the swapping loop,  
for each `i` (`0 ≤ i < n/2`) `arr[i]` contains the node that was originally
at position `n-1-i` of the list and `arr[n-1-i]` contains the node that
was originally at position `i`.

**Proof.**  
The loop iterates `i` from `0` to `n/2-1` and performs a single swap
of `arr[i]` and `arr[n-1-i]`.  
These pairs are disjoint and cover exactly all indices,
therefore each node is moved to the opposite side of the array,
which corresponds to the opposite position in the list. ∎



##### Lemma 4  
After rebuilding the `next` pointers according to the swapped array,
the new list order is exactly the required swapped order.

**Proof.**  
The rebuilding step sets `arr[i]->next = arr[i+1]` for all `i < n-1` and
`arr[n-1]->next = NULL`.  
Consequently the list starting at `arr[0]` follows the sequence
`arr[0] → arr[1] → … → arr[n-1]`.  
By Lemma&nbsp;3 this sequence is exactly the list with the first element
swapped with the last, second with the second‑last, etc. ∎



##### Theorem  
The algorithm outputs the data of the singly linked list after swapping
first with last, second with second‑last, etc.  
If the input list is empty it outputs `NULL`.

**Proof.**  
If the list is empty we output `NULL` immediately – correct by the statement.  
Otherwise, by Lemma&nbsp;1 the initial list has the input order.  
Lemmas&nbsp;2–4 show that the rebuilding step produces a list that is the
required swapped list.  
The final traversal prints the data of this list in order, therefore the
output matches the specification. ∎



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Complexity Analysis  

Let `n` be the number of valid integers.

*Building the list* – `O(n)` time, `O(n)` memory.  
*Collecting pointers* – `O(n)` time, `O(n)` memory.  
*Swapping* – `O(n)` time, `O(1)` extra memory.  
*Rebuilding* – `O(n)` time.  
*Printing* – `O(n)` time.

Overall:  
**Time** `O(n)`  
**Memory** `O(n)` (list + pointer array).

Both satisfy the limits (1 s, 64 MB).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Reference Implementation (C 99)

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <errno.h>

/* Node of singly linked list */
struct node {
    int data;
    struct node *next;
};

int main(void)
{
    struct node *head = NULL;
    struct node *tail = NULL;

    /* ---------- read input ---------- */
    char token[128];
    while (scanf("%127s", token) == 1) {
        if (strcmp(token, ".") == 0)            /* end of sequence */
            break;

        char *endptr;
        errno = 0;
        long val = strtol(token, &endptr, 10);

        /* token is not a valid integer -> ignore it */
        if (*endptr != '\0' || errno != 0)
            continue;

        /* create a new node */
        struct node *new = malloc(sizeof(struct node));
        if (!new) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Memory allocation failed\n");
            return 1;
        }
        new->data = (int)val;
        new->next = NULL;

        if (!head) {          /* first element */
            head = tail = new;
        } else {              /* append to the end */
            tail->next = new;
            tail = new;
        }
    }

    /* ---------- empty list ---------- */
    if (!head) {
        printf("NULL\n");
        return 0;
    }

    /* ---------- collect pointers ---------- */
    size_t n = 0;
    for (struct node *p = head; p; p = p->next)
        ++n;

    struct node **arr = malloc(n * sizeof(struct node *));
    if (!arr) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Memory allocation failed\n");
        return 1;
    }

    size_t idx = 0;
    for (struct node *p = head; p; p = p->next)
        arr[idx++] = p;

    /* ---------- swap nodes in array ---------- */
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n / 2; ++i) {
        struct node *tmp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[n - 1 - i];
        arr[n - 1 - i] = tmp;
    }

    /* ---------- rebuild linked list ---------- */
    for (size_t i = 0; i + 1 < n; ++i)
        arr[i]->next = arr[i + 1];
    arr[n - 1]->next = NULL;
    head = arr[0];
    free(arr);

    /* ---------- output ---------- */
    for (struct node *p = head; p; p = p->next) {
        if (p != head) putchar(' ');
        printf("%d", p->data);
    }
    putchar('\n');

    /* ---------- clean up ---------- */
    for (struct node *p = head; p; ) {
        struct node *next = p->next;
        free(p);
        p = next;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

The program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and
conforms to the C 99 standard.

