## 4. Practical notes / pitfalls

| Issue | Explanation | Fix / Recommendation |
|-------|-------------|----------------------|
| **Hard‑coded port 1024** | Port 1024 is the first non‑privileged port, but many OSes block it or it might already be in use. | Allow port to be passed as a command‑line argument or pick a random free port (`0`). |
| **ObjectInputStream per read** | The code creates a new `ObjectInputStream` for each read. This causes a new stream header to be expected every time, which will throw an exception if the client writes a single `ObjectOutputStream` header at the start of the connection. | Reuse a single `ObjectInputStream` per socket, or use `ObjectInputStream` only once at the beginning. |
| **Partial reads** | `InputStream.read(byte[])` may return fewer than 1024 bytes even when the client has sent a full object. The code assumes a *complete* object is read in one call. | Either use a `BufferedInputStream` + loop until you get a full object, or use `ObjectInputStream` directly on the socket stream. |
| **No stream flushing** | After writing a `ByteArrayOutputStream` to the `PrintStream`, the data may not be sent until the stream is flushed. | Call `ps.flush()` after `ps.write(responseBuffer);`. |
| **Exception handling** | The server simply prints the stack trace and continues; the client thread may terminate silently. | Consider logging more gracefully or informing the client of an error. |
| **Thread pool size** | Fixed at 10. If more clients connect, they’ll block on `pool.execute(...)`. | Either increase the pool size or use a cached or scheduled pool. |
| **Closing resources** | `ObjectInputStream` and `ObjectOutputStream` are not closed; they’re created per message. | Ensure they’re closed or reused properly. |

