### Short answer  
No – Java has no built‑in way to *force* a method to receive a deep copy of an object.  
When you pass an object reference to a method Java always passes **the reference itself by value** (i.e. a copy of the pointer). The object that the reference points to is *not* duplicated automatically.  

If you need a deep copy you must create it yourself (or rely on a library). Below is a quick rundown of what that means, why it matters, and how you can do it.

---

## 1. What “pass‑by‑value” really means in Java

| Primitive type | Passed? | Result |
|-----------------|--------|--------|
| `int`, `double`, … | yes | The numeric value is copied. |
| `Object` (e.g. `String`, `List`, user class) | yes | The **reference** is copied. |

So if you write:

```java
void foo(List<String> list) {
    // ...
}
```

- The method receives a **copy of the reference** to the same `List` object.
- Inside `foo`, you can modify the contents of the list (`list.add(...)`).
- Those modifications are visible to the caller because both the caller and the callee point to the *same* object.

```java
List<String> myList = new ArrayList<>();
foo(myList);      // myList still points to the same ArrayList
```

The reference itself cannot be made immutable by Java’s calling semantics.

---

## 2. When you actually *do* want a deep copy

You might need a deep copy when:

| Scenario | Why you need a deep copy |
|----------|--------------------------|
| **Encapsulation / defensive programming** | You want to protect the original object from accidental changes. |
| **Immutability** | You expose an object to callers but want to guarantee it won’t change. |
| **Thread safety** | Two threads need separate copies of a mutable state. |
| **Serialization/remote calls** | Data is sent over a network or written to disk; you want a snapshot. |

### 2.1 Manual copy (copy constructor / clone)

```java
class Person {
    String name;
    Address address;          // Address is a mutable, nested object

    Person(Person other) {     // copy constructor
        this.name = other.name;            // shallow copy of primitives/strings
        this.address = new Address(other.address); // deep copy of nested
    }
}
```

```java
Person original = new Person(...);
Person copy = new Person(original); // deep copy
```

### 2.2 `Cloneable` interface

```java
class Person implements Cloneable {
    // fields …

    @Override
    public Person clone() {
        try {
            Person copy = (Person) super.clone(); // shallow copy
            copy.address = address.clone();       // deep copy nested objects
            return copy;
        } catch (CloneNotSupportedException e) {
            throw new AssertionError();
        }
    }
}
```

> **Tip:** `Cloneable` is considered error‑prone; many Java developers avoid it in favor of copy constructors or factory methods.

### 2.3 Serialization‑based deep copy (simple but expensive)

```java
public static <T> T deepCopyViaSerialization(T object) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
    ByteArrayOutputStream bos = new ByteArrayOutputStream();
    try (ObjectOutputStream out = new ObjectOutputStream(bos)) {
        out.writeObject(object);
    }

    ByteArrayInputStream bis = new ByteArrayInputStream(bos.toByteArray());
    try (ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(bis)) {
        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        T copy = (T) in.readObject();
        return copy;
    }
}
```

All classes involved must implement `Serializable`. This is handy for quick prototypes but adds runtime overhead and doesn’t handle non‑serializable fields.

### 2.4 Third‑party libraries

- **Apache Commons Lang** (`SerializationUtils.clone(...)`)
- **Object‑Graph‑Mapper** (OGM) or **Joda‑Beans** for immutable beans
- **Jackson** (JSON serialization/deserialization)
- **CGLIB** or **ByteBuddy** for runtime bytecode manipulation

---

## 3. Defensive copying in method signatures

If you *control* the method that receives the object, the simplest way to guarantee the callee works with a copy is:

```java
void foo(List<String> list) {
    List<String> copy = new ArrayList<>(list);   // shallow copy (deep if elements are immutable)
    // use `copy` safely
}
```

If you don’t own the method, you can still create a copy *before* calling it:

```java
List<String> original = ...
List<String> copy = new ArrayList<>(original);
foo(copy);
```

---

## 4. Immutability – the safest alternative

If you can design your data to be immutable (no setters, final fields, defensive copies in constructors), you get the benefits of deep copies “for free”:

```java
final class Person {
    private final String name;
    private final Address address; // Address itself is immutable

    // constructor, getters, no setters
}
```

Immutable objects are inherently thread‑safe and safe to share without copying. For many APIs, especially APIs that return collections, Java already provides immutable views (e.g. `Collections.unmodifiableList`).

---

## 5. Bottom line

| Need | How to get it | Caveats |
|------|--------------|---------|
| **True deep copy of arbitrary objects** | Implement copy constructors / clone methods / serialization / libraries | Must handle every field; expensive if many nested objects |
| **Safe usage inside a method** | Defensive copy at entry point | Shallow vs deep depends on element types |
| **Unmodifiable, thread‑safe data** | Make classes immutable | Requires design upfront |

> **Key takeaway**: Java’s parameter passing model does not offer “pass by value” of the object contents. If a deep copy is required, you must explicitly create it. The best practice is to design your classes to be immutable or provide clear copy mechanisms.

--- 

### Quick cheat‑sheet

```java
// 1. Copy constructor
class A {
    int x;
    B nested; // mutable

    A(A other) { this.x = other.x; this.nested = new B(other.nested); }
}

// 2. Cloneable (avoid if possible)
class B implements Cloneable { /* ... */ }

// 3. Serialization
T copy = SerializationUtils.clone(original);

// 4. Immutability
final class C { /* final fields only */ }

// 5. Defensive copy inside method
void foo(List<String> list) {
    List<String> copy = new ArrayList<>(list);
    // ...
}
```

