## 1.  Why “Networking & Web Services” in Java?

- **Connectivity** – Every distributed application needs to talk to other systems (other Java apps, legacy services, mobile clients, etc.).  
- **Inter‑process communication (IPC)** – From low‑level sockets to high‑level HTTP/REST/SOAP.  
- **Platform neutrality** – Java’s networking APIs are part of the JDK, so you can run the same code on Windows, macOS, Linux, or a cloud VM.  
- **Ecosystem** – There are mature, battle‑tested libraries (e.g., Apache HttpClient, OkHttp, Spring WebFlux) and standards (JAX‑WS, JAX‑RS, gRPC).  

Below is a “quick‑start” guide that walks through the most common patterns and code snippets you’ll see in real projects.

---

## 2.  Core JDK Networking APIs

| Category | Typical Use | Key Classes / Interfaces | Java 9+ Feature |
|----------|-------------|--------------------------|-----------------|
| **TCP/UDP Sockets** | Raw byte streams, custom protocols | `Socket`, `ServerSocket`, `DatagramSocket`, `DatagramPacket` | NIO (`SocketChannel`, `DatagramChannel`) |
| **HTTP Client** | REST calls, simple web scraping | `java.net.URL`, `HttpURLConnection` | `java.net.http.HttpClient` (Java 11) |
| **URI / URL parsing** | Parsing addresses | `URI`, `URL` | N/A |
| **WebSocket** | Real‑time duplex communication | `java.net.http.WebSocket` (Java 11) | N/A |
| **Multicast** | Group messaging | `MulticastSocket` | N/A |

### 2.1 Example: Simple TCP Echo Server / Client

```java
// EchoServer.java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;

public class EchoServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        try (ServerSocket ss = new ServerSocket(9999)) {
            System.out.println("Server listening on 9999");
            while (true) {
                Socket client = ss.accept();
                new Thread(() -> handle(client)).start();
            }
        }
    }

    private static void handle(Socket socket) {
        try (socket;
             BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream()));
             PrintWriter out  = new PrintWriter(socket.getOutputStream(), true)) {

            String line;
            while ((line = in.readLine()) != null) {
                System.out.println("Client says: " + line);
                out.println("Echo: " + line);
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
