## TL;DR

`java.io.FileNotFoundException` when you hit  
`https://ipnpb.sandbox.paypal.com/cgi-bin/webscr` is almost always a **HTTP 404** that PayPal is returning because your listener is not talking to it the way PayPal expects.

* **You’re probably using a GET** (or a POST without the required `cmd=_notify-validate` prefix).  
* Or you’re pointing at the wrong URL (typo, missing trailing slash, or the old `www.paypal.com` endpoint that PayPal has retired).

Fix it by:

1. **POST back the exact IPN message you received** + `cmd=_notify-validate` **to the URL**  
   `https://ipnpb.sandbox.paypal.com/cgi-bin/webscr` (or `https://ipnpb.paypal.com/cgi-bin/webscr` for production).
2. **Use an HTTPS connection** (cast to `HttpsURLConnection` or the newer `HttpClient`).
3. **Check the HTTP status** before reading the stream – a 404 means PayPal rejected the request.

Below is a complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready Java 8+ example (using the legacy `HttpURLConnection`), plus a modern `HttpClient` snippet for Java 11+.  
Pick the one that matches your environment.

---

## Why PayPal Returns 404

| Situation | What PayPal Does | Why You See `FileNotFoundException` |
|-----------|------------------|-------------------------------------|
| GET request to `/cgi-bin/webscr` | Returns 404 (PayPal’s IPN endpoint is *only* for POST). | `HttpURLConnection.getInputStream()` throws `FileNotFoundException` on 404. |
| POST without `cmd=_notify-validate` | Returns 404 (PayPal expects that parameter). | Same reason. |
| Wrong host (e.g. typo, old URL) | 404 or DNS failure. | Same reason. |
| HTTPS but you use plain `URL` without proper SSL context | SSL handshake failure, not a 404. | Would see `SSLHandshakeException`. |
| Network issue (firewall, proxy, etc.) | No connection, not 404. | Would see `UnknownHostException` or `ConnectException`. |

So, in almost all real‑world cases of that exception, you’re hitting a **404**.

---

## 1. Classic `HttpURLConnection` (Java 8‑)

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;

public class PaypalIpnValidator {

    private static final String PAYPAL_SANDBOX_URL =
            "https://ipnpb.sandbox.paypal.com/cgi-bin/webscr";

    /**
     * Validate the IPN message that you received from PayPal.
     *
     * @param ipnParams the raw POST body that you got from PayPal
     * @return true if PayPal replies "VERIFIED", false otherwise
     * @throws IOException if network or I/O errors occur
     */
    public boolean validate(String ipnParams) throws IOException {

        // Prepend cmd=_notify-validate to the original message
        String payload = "cmd=_notify-validate&" + ipnParams;

        // Build HTTPS connection
        URL url = new URL(PAYPAL_SANDBOX_URL);
        HttpsURLConnection conn = (HttpsURLConnection) url.openConnection();
        conn.setRequestMethod("POST");
        conn.setDoOutput(true);
        conn.setUseCaches(false);

        // PayPal requires the content type to be form‑urlencoded
        conn.setRequestProperty("Content-Type", "application/x-www-form-urlencoded");
        conn.setRequestProperty("Connection", "Close");

        // Write the payload
        try (OutputStream out = conn.getOutputStream()) {
            out.write(payload.getBytes("UTF-8"));
        }

        // Get PayPal’s response code – 200 OK means PayPal processed it
        int responseCode = conn.getResponseCode();
        if (responseCode != 200) {
            // 404 or any non‑200 will be caught here
            // You can log conn.getResponseMessage() if you like
            return false;
        }

        // Read PayPal’s body (VERIFIED / INVALID)
        try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(
                new InputStreamReader(conn.getInputStream(), "UTF-8"))) {

            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            String line;
            while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) {
                sb.append(line);
            }

            String response = sb.toString().trim();
            return "VERIFIED".equalsIgnoreCase(response);
        }
    }
}
```

### Common Gotchas in the Code

| Symptom | Fix |
|---------|-----|
| `FileNotFoundException: https://ipnpb.sandbox.paypal.com/...` | Verify `responseCode` is 200. If not, the request is malformed. |
| `IOException: HTTPS hostname wrong` | Ensure `https://` prefix and that you’re not behind a proxy that hijacks TLS. |
| `java.lang.UnsupportedOperationException` when writing to `getOutputStream()` | Call `conn.setDoOutput(true)` before writing. |

---

## 2. Modern Java 11+ `HttpClient`

If you’re on Java 11 or later, you can use the new `java.net.http.HttpClient`. It’s cleaner and handles HTTPS out of the box.

```java
import java.net.URI;
import java.net.http.*;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;

public class PaypalIpnValidator {

    private static final String PAYPAL_SANDBOX_URL =
            "https://ipnpb.sandbox.paypal.com/cgi-bin/webscr";

    public boolean validate(String ipnParams) throws Exception {
        String payload = "cmd=_notify-validate&" + ipnParams;

        HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
                .followRedirects(HttpClient.Redirect.NEVER) // PayPal won’t redirect, but just in case
                .build();

        HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
                .uri(URI.create(PAYPAL_SANDBOX_URL))
                .header("Content-Type", "application/x-www-form-urlencoded")
                .POST(HttpRequest.BodyPublishers.ofString(payload, StandardCharsets.UTF_8))
                .build();

        HttpResponse<String> response = client.send(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());

        if (response.statusCode() != 200) {
            // non‑200 response; log response.body() if needed
            return false;
        }

        return "VERIFIED".equalsIgnoreCase(response.body().trim());
    }
}
```

---

## 3. Quick Checklist to Avoid the Exception

| ✔️ | Item | Why it matters |
|----|------|----------------|
| 1 | **URL** | `https://ipnpb.sandbox.paypal.com/cgi-bin/webscr` (or `https://ipnpb.paypal.com/cgi-bin/webscr` for live). |
| 2 | **Method** | `POST` only. |
| 3 | **Body** | `cmd=_notify-validate` **plus** the *exact* raw body you received (no URL‑encoding of `&` or `=`). |
| 4 | **Content‑Type** | `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`. |
| 5 | **SSL/TLS** | Use `HttpsURLConnection` or Java 11 `HttpClient`; ensure JVM trusts PayPal’s cert (most JDKs already have it). |
| 6 | **Read status first** | Call `getResponseCode()` or `response.statusCode()` before trying to read the body. |
| 7 | **Logging** | Log the raw IPN message and PayPal’s response (`VERIFIED`/`INVALID`) for auditability. |

---

## 4. Debugging Steps

1. **Print the HTTP status**  
   ```java
   int code = conn.getResponseCode();
   System.out.println("PayPal returned: " + code);
   ```
   If you get `404`, the request format is wrong.

2. **Inspect the raw request**  
   Log `payload` before sending. It should look like:  
   ```
   cmd=_notify-validate&txn_id=XYZ...&payment_status=Completed&...
   ```

3. **Check for proxies or firewalls**  
   If you’re behind a corporate proxy, you may need to set `-Dhttps.proxyHost` and `-Dhttps.proxyPort`.

4. **Test with curl**  
   ```bash
   curl -v -X POST "https://ipnpb.sandbox.paypal.com/cgi-bin/webscr" \
        -H "Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded" \
        --data-urlencode "cmd=_notify-validate&<your IPN body>"
   ```
   If curl gets 404, the problem is in the body or URL, not your Java code.

---

## 5. Common “why is this still 404?” scenarios

| Scenario | Likely Cause | Quick Fix |
|----------|--------------|-----------|
| You’re using `https://ipnpb.paypal.com/...` in *sandbox* mode. | Wrong host for sandbox. | Use the sandbox URL (`ipnpb.sandbox.paypal.com`). |
| The POST body contains `&` characters that have been URL‑encoded twice. | Double‑encoding corrupts the request. | Do **not** encode the `&` or `=` when concatenating the original IPN message. |
| You’re sending `cmd=_notify-validate` *without* the original IPN fields. | PayPal expects the entire original message. | Append the original body exactly after `cmd=_notify-validate&`. |
| You’re using `URLConnection` but forgot to call `setDoOutput(true)`. | Connection is in “GET” mode. | Call `conn.setDoOutput(true)` before writing. |
| Your JVM has an **out‑of‑date trust store** that cannot validate PayPal’s cert. | SSL handshake fails, not 404, but you might mis‑interpret. | Update `cacerts` or use a recent JDK. |

---

## 6. Final Words

- The *FileNotFoundException* is a classic `HttpURLConnection` symptom of a 404 status.  
- PayPal’s IPN endpoint is *strict*: only a POST with `cmd=_notify-validate` and the raw body is accepted.  
- Modern Java (`HttpClient`) reduces boilerplate and avoids many of the pitfalls of the old `URL`/`HttpURLConnection` APIs.  
- Always log the full IPN payload and PayPal’s reply for troubleshooting and audit trails.

Once you hit the checklist above, the 404 should vanish, and your listener will reliably validate IPNs. Happy coding!

