Below is a **minimal, self‑contained Java program** that:

1. Compiles a tiny “Hello World” Java class (or any other class you give it).  
2. Packages that class into a runnable JAR.  
3. Calls the GraalVM **native‑image** tool to produce a native Windows EXE (or Linux‑ELF / macOS Mach‑O binary on those platforms).  

> **Why this is useful**  
>  * If you already have a Gradle/Maven build you can drop this into a CI step or a tiny helper tool.  
>  * It works entirely from Java code – no shell script, no external build file – so it can be embedded in any Java‑based automation tool.  
>  * It demonstrates the exact command line that GraalVM uses, which you can adapt to your own projects.

---

## 1. Prerequisites

| Item | How to get it | Notes |
|------|---------------|-------|
| **GraalVM (JDK)** | [Download GraalVM CE](https://github.com/graalvm/graalvm-ce-builds/releases) for your platform, unzip it and add it to your `PATH`. | The JDK version must match the version you’ll use for the Java code you want to compile. |
| **native‑image** | Run `gu install native-image` (the GraalVM updater). | Installs the native‑image tool into the same GraalVM installation. |
| **Java source** | Place the Java source you want to build in `src/main/java/` relative to the program’s working directory. | The example uses a simple Hello‑World class called `com.example.Hello`. |

> **Tip:** On Windows you’ll see `native-image.exe` automatically; on Linux/macOS the executable will be named `native-image`.  
>  The native‑image command automatically picks the right name for the binary – just add `-H:Name=MyApp` and it will create `MyApp.exe` (Windows) or `MyApp` (others).

---

## 2. The Java Automation Code

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class GraalNativeBuilder {

    // Change this to your main class fully‑qualified name
    private static final String MAIN_CLASS = "com.example.Hello";

    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        // 1. Compile the source
        compileSources();

        // 2. Package into a runnable jar
        String jarPath = packageJar();

        // 3. Build the native image
        buildNativeImage(jarPath);

        System.out.println("✅ Native image created!");
    }

    /* --------------------- 1. Compile --------------------- */
    private static void compileSources() throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        System.out.println("[1/3] Compiling Java sources...");

        // javac -d out src/main/java/**/*.java
        List<String> javacCmd = new ArrayList<>();
        javacCmd.add("javac");
        javacCmd.add("-d");
        javacCmd.add("out");

        // Find all .java files under src/main/java
        Files.walk(Paths.get("src/main/java"))
             .filter(p -> p.toString().endsWith(".java"))
             .forEach(p -> javacCmd.add(p.toString()));

        Process p = new ProcessBuilder(javacCmd)
                .inheritIO()
                .redirectErrorStream(true)
                .start();

        if (p.waitFor() != 0) {
            throw new RuntimeException("javac failed");
        }
    }

    /* --------------------- 2. Package --------------------- */
    private static String packageJar() throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        System.out.println("[2/3] Creating runnable jar...");

        // Ensure target directory exists
        Files.createDirectories(Paths.get("target"));

        // jar -cfm target/app.jar manifest.txt -C out .
        // We'll build a minimal manifest that sets Main-Class
        Path manifest = Paths.get("manifest.txt");
        Files.writeString(manifest, "Main-Class: " + MAIN_CLASS + System.lineSeparator());

        List<String> jarCmd = new ArrayList<>();
        jarCmd.add("jar");
        jarCmd.add("-cfm");
        jarCmd.add("target/app.jar");
        jarCmd.add("manifest.txt");
        jarCmd.add("-C");
        jarCmd.add("out");
        jarCmd.add(".");

        Process p = new ProcessBuilder(jarCmd)
                .inheritIO()
                .redirectErrorStream(true)
                .start();

        if (p.waitFor() != 0) {
            throw new RuntimeException("jar failed");
        }

        // Clean up
        Files.deleteIfExists(manifest);

        return "target/app.jar";
    }

    /* --------------------- 3. Native Image --------------------- */
    private static void buildNativeImage(String jarPath) throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        System.out.println("[3/3] Building native image...");

        // On Windows we want *.exe, on other OSes the name is the same
        String exeName = "myapp";
        if (System.getProperty("os.name").toLowerCase().contains("win")) {
            exeName += ".exe";
        }

        List<String> imgCmd = new ArrayList<>();
        imgCmd.add("native-image");                // assumes native-image is on PATH
        imgCmd.add("-jar");
        imgCmd.add(jarPath);
        imgCmd.add("-H:Name=" + exeName);          // output binary name
        imgCmd.add("--no-fallback");               // optional: remove fallback image
        imgCmd.add("-H:+StaticExecutableWithDynamicLibraries"); // optional

        Process p = new ProcessBuilder(imgCmd)
                .inheritIO()
                .redirectErrorStream(true)
                .start();

        int exit = p.waitFor();
        if (exit != 0) {
            throw new RuntimeException("native-image exited with code " + exit);
        }
    }
}
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens | Key command |
|------|--------------|-------------|
| 1 | `javac -d out <source files>` | Compiles all `.java` files into the `out` directory. |
| 2 | `jar -cfm target/app.jar manifest.txt -C out .` | Packages the classes into a runnable JAR with a minimal manifest that points to `MAIN_CLASS`. |
| 3 | `native-image -jar target/app.jar -H:Name=myapp` | Calls GraalVM’s native‑image tool to build a statically linked native binary. The `-H:Name=` option controls the file name. |

> **Cross‑platform note**  
> The code automatically adds `.exe` for Windows. On Linux or macOS the binary will be named `myapp`. If you want a different name just change the `exeName` variable.

---

## 3. Running the automation

```bash
# 1. Make sure GraalVM (JDK) is on PATH and native-image is installed
$ gu --version
# 2. Build the native image
$ javac GraalNativeBuilder.java
$ java GraalNativeBuilder
```

If everything succeeds you’ll find:

* `out/` – compiled class files  
* `target/app.jar` – runnable JAR  
* `myapp.exe` (Windows) or `myapp` (Linux/macOS) – the native executable

Run the executable:

```bash
# On Linux / macOS
$ ./myapp
Hello, world!

# On Windows
> myapp.exe
Hello, world!
```

---

## 4. Customising for your own project

| What to change | How |
|----------------|-----|
| **Main class** | Edit `MAIN_CLASS` at the top of the file. |
| **Source location** | The script assumes sources live under `src/main/java`. If yours lives elsewhere, change the `Files.walk(Paths.get("src/main/java"))` part. |
| **Build flags** | Add any `native-image` options you need (e.g. `--enable-all-security-services`, `--no-fallback`, `-J-Xmx1g`, etc.) in the `imgCmd` list. |
| **Jar name** | Change `"target/app.jar"` to wherever you want the intermediate JAR. |
| **Executable name** | Change `exeName` or compute it dynamically from your main class. |

---

## 5. Alternative: Using the GraalVM Gradle / Maven plugins

If you already use Gradle or Maven you can get the same result without writing Java code:

* **Gradle** (plugin `org.graalvm.nativeimage`):  
  ```groovy
  plugins {
      id "org.graalvm.nativeimage" version "0.9.18"
  }
  nativeImage {
      mainClass = 'com.example.Hello'
      outputName = 'myapp'
      buildArgs = ['--no-fallback']
  }
  ```

* **Maven** (plugin `org.graalvm.native-image-maven-plugin`):  
  ```xml
  <build>
      <plugins>
          <plugin>
              <groupId>org.graalvm.nativeimage-maven-plugin</groupId>
              <artifactId>native-image-maven-plugin</artifactId>
              <version>0.9.18</version>
              <executions>
                  <execution>
                      <goals>
                          <goal>native-image</goal>
                      </goals>
                  </execution>
              </executions>
          </plugin>
      </plugins>
  </build>
  ```

Use the Java helper above if you need to drive the build **from inside another Java application** or want a platform‑agnostic, script‑free approach.

---

## 6. Common pitfalls

| Issue | Why it happens | Fix |
|-------|----------------|-----|
| `native-image` not found | GraalVM’s `bin` directory isn’t on `PATH` | Add `PATH` to the GraalVM `bin` directory or give the full path to `native-image`. |
| `native-image` complains about “No bootstrap jar found” | The JDK isn’t a GraalVM JDK or `native-image` is run with the wrong JDK | Ensure the Java runtime used to launch the program is the same GraalVM JDK that contains `native-image`. |
| Fallback image created instead of native exe | `--no-fallback` missing and some feature needs a fallback | Either add `--no-fallback` or keep the fallback (it will produce a `.jar` fallback). |
| Slow compilation | `native-image` needs to resolve reflection / dynamic class loading | Add `-H:ReflectionConfigurationFiles=reflection-config.json` or other config files if needed. |

---

## 7. Wrap‑up

This Java program gives you:

* A **single, reusable Java class** that does everything.  
* No external build scripts – just standard JDK tools (`javac`, `jar`) and GraalVM’s `native-image`.  
* Full control over the native‑image command line, so you can add extra flags, security options, or build‑time configurations.

Feel free to adapt the code to your own directory layout or CI environment. Happy building!

