Below is a straight‑forward translation of the three JavaScript helper functions into **Android/Java** code.  
The Java code works on a `List<PointF>` (the floating‑point version of the Android `Point` class).  
If you prefer integers or a custom data‑structure, just swap out the `PointF` type.

```java
import android.graphics.PointF;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Utility class that smoothes a polyline by repeatedly averaging neighbouring points.
 */
public final class TrailSmoother {

    /**
     * Smooth the supplied list of points a given number of times.
     *
     * @param points the original list of points (may be null – in which case the method
     *               will simply return null).
     * @param iterations number of times to apply the averaging filter.
     * @return a new list containing the smoothed points.
     */
    public static List<PointF> fittingTrailsMultiple(List<PointF> points, int iterations) {
        if (points == null) {
            return null;
        }

        List<PointF> newPoints = new ArrayList<>(points);
        for (int i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
            newPoints = fittingTrails(newPoints);
        }
        return newPoints;
    }

    /**
     * Apply a single smoothing pass.
     *
     * @param points the input list (must contain at least 5 points – otherwise the method
     *               will simply copy the original list).
     * @return a new list containing the smoothed points.
     */
    public static List<PointF> fittingTrails(List<PointF> points) {
        // Defensive copy – we don't want to modify the original list
        List<PointF> newPoints = new ArrayList<>(points.size());

        for (int i = 0; i < points.size(); i++) {
            if (i > 2 && i < points.size() - 2) {
                newPoints.add(pointsAvg(points, i));
            } else {
                // keep the first 3 and the last 2 points unchanged
                newPoints.add(points.get(i));
            }
        }

        return newPoints;
    }

    /**
     * Compute the average of five consecutive points centred at the given index.
     *
     * @param points the list of points (must be at least 5 elements long).
     * @param i the centre index (must be >2 and < points.size()-2).
     * @return a new PointF holding the average coordinates.
     */
    public static PointF pointsAvg(List<PointF> points, int i) {
        // The JavaScript version used parseInt on the key.  Here we already have an int index.
        float x = 0;
        float y = 0;

        // sum the five neighbours (+ the centre)
        for (int offset = -2; offset <= 2; offset++) {
            PointF p = points.get(i + offset);
            x += p.x;
            y += p.y;
        }

        return new PointF(x / 5f, y / 5f);
    }
}
```

