Below is a **complete, minimal, and secure‑ready** example that shows how to:

1. Capture the form parameters (as you already do).  
2. Store them as cookies.  
3. **Forward the same request to `/Welcome`** so that the *Welcome* servlet’s `doPost` is executed automatically – the HTTP method remains *POST*.  

> **Why a forward and not a `sendRedirect`?**  
> `sendRedirect` tells the browser to issue a *new* request (normally a *GET*).  
> If you want the *POST* data and the same request object to reach the next servlet, use a forward (`RequestDispatcher.forward`).  
> The container keeps the request/response objects untouched and simply dispatches them to the target servlet, so the `doPost` of `/Welcome` will be invoked automatically.

---

## 1️⃣  Your updated servlet (e.g. `/Register`)

```java
import jakarta.servlet.ServletException;
import jakarta.servlet.http.*;
import jakarta.servlet.annotation.WebServlet;
import java.io.IOException;

@WebServlet("/Register")
public class RegisterServlet extends HttpServlet {

    @Override
    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest req,
                          HttpServletResponse resp) throws ServletException, IOException {

        // 1️⃣  Grab the parameters
        String vName        = req.getParameter("username");
        String vPass        = req.getParameter("password");
        String vGender      = req.getParameter("gender");
        String vEmail       = req.getParameter("email");
        String vPhoneNumber = req.getParameter("phone");

        // 2️⃣  (Optional)  Log them – useful for debugging
        System.out.println("username: " + vName);
        System.out.println("password: " + vPass);
        System.out.println("gender  : " + vGender);
        System.out.println("email   : " + vEmail);
        System.out.println("phone   : " + vPhoneNumber);

        // 3️⃣  Persist each value in a cookie
        int shortExpiry = 60 * 60 * 24; // 24 h
        createCookie(resp, "username",  vName,        shortExpiry);
        createCookie(resp, "password",  vPass,        shortExpiry);
        createCookie(resp, "gender",    vGender,      shortExpiry);
        createCookie(resp, "email",     vEmail,       shortExpiry);
        createCookie(resp, "phoneNum",  vPhoneNumber, shortExpiry);

        // 4️⃣  Forward the *same* request to /Welcome
        //     The request will hit WelcomeServlet#doPost automatically.
        req.getRequestDispatcher("/Welcome")
           .forward(req, resp);   // <--- THIS IS THE KEY LINE
    }

    /** Helper that creates a cookie and adds it to the response */
    private void createCookie(HttpServletResponse resp,
                              String name, String value, int expiry) {

        // For security: mark it HttpOnly and SameSite
        Cookie cookie = new Cookie(name, value);
        cookie.setMaxAge(expiry);
        cookie.setHttpOnly(true);          // prevents JS access
        cookie.setPath("/");               // accessible to all paths
        // Optional: SameSite=Lax/Strict if you use Servlet 4.0+
        resp.addCookie(cookie);
    }
}
```

### Quick sanity checks

| Item | Why it matters | Where it is in the code |
|------|----------------|------------------------|
| **Same method (POST)** | The Welcome servlet can rely on `doPost` to receive the form data. | `req.getRequestDispatcher(...).forward(req, resp);` |
| **Same request/response objects** | Allows the Welcome servlet to read cookies you just set (via `request.getCookies()` or `request.getCookie(name)`). | Same request forwarded. |
| **No extra round‑trip** | Faster, no new HTTP request/response cycle. | Forward, not redirect. |

---

## 2️⃣  The `/Welcome` servlet (what will be executed)

```java
import jakarta.servlet.ServletException;
import jakarta.servlet.http.*;
import jakarta.servlet.annotation.WebServlet;
import java.io.IOException;

@WebServlet("/Welcome")
public class WelcomeServlet extends HttpServlet {

    @Override
    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest req,
                          HttpServletResponse resp) throws ServletException, IOException {

        // Example: read the cookies you just set
        String user = getCookieValue(req, "username");
        // ... (other logic)

        resp.setContentType("text/html;charset=UTF-8");
        resp.getWriter().println("<h1>Welcome, " + user + "!</h1>");
    }

    private String getCookieValue(HttpServletRequest req, String name) {
        if (req.getCookies() == null) return null;
        for (Cookie c : req.getCookies()) {
            if (c.getName().equals(name)) {
                return c.getValue();
            }
        }
        return null;
    }
}
```

