## What’s going on

You’re hitting Spring’s *Whitelabel Error Page* – a generic 500 response that Spring shows when it can’t find an `/error` handler and a real exception bubbles up somewhere in your stack.  
In this particular project the most common culprits are:

| Issue | Why it causes a 500 | Fix |
|-------|---------------------|-----|
| **Wrong quote characters** (`“ ”` instead of `" "`) | They’re not Java string delimiters – the compiler will treat them as “Unicode” quotes and you’ll get a *syntax error* (the app simply won’t start). | Replace every `“` / `”` with the normal ASCII `"`. |
| **MySQL driver class name** | Spring Boot 3+ ships with MySQL Connector/J 8 which uses the class `com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver`. Using `com.mysql.jdbc.Driver` will cause the DataSource to fail to initialise, leading to an `IllegalStateException: Failed to determine a suitable driver class`. | Update the property to `spring.datasource.driver-class-name=com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver`. |
| **Missing MySQL connector dependency** | Without the connector JAR Spring can’t create a JDBC connection – the application fails on start. | Add `mysql:mysql-connector-j` to `pom.xml` (or `build.gradle`). |
| **No JPA entity or repository package scan** | Spring will start but when it tries to use `UserRepository` the `EntityManager` can’t find the entity, throwing an exception. | Ensure `@Entity` is on `User` and that it and the repository are in a package scanned by Spring (same package or sub‑package of the main `@SpringBootApplication`). |
| **Missing view templates** | Returning `"user-list"` or `"user-create"` tells Spring to render a Thymeleaf (or other view) template that doesn’t exist, throwing `ServletException: Could not find template`. | Add the corresponding `.html` files under `src/main/resources/templates/`. |
| **Database unreachable** | Even with the correct driver, wrong URL, username, password or a firewall will cause a connection failure at runtime. | Verify the URL, credentials, and that MySQL is running on `localhost:3306`. |

Below is a *cleaned‑up* version of the code that addresses the above points, plus a quick `application.properties` that works out‑of‑the‑box for most local dev setups.

---

