Below is a **complete, self‑contained example** that shows how you can:

1. Detect every “root” (drive) that is visible to Java  
2. Recursively walk each drive and build a lightweight index (path → file‑meta)  
3. Persist that index to disk (JSON)  
4. Use the persisted index to answer “find this file” queries without a full‑scan  
5. Keep the index up‑to‑date by watching every directory that you indexed

The code is written for **Java 17+** (you only need the JDK, no external dependencies – although a single‑file JSON library is optional, here we use the minimal `org.json` which can be dropped in as a single source file if you don’t want Maven).

---

## 1.  Detecting the roots

```java
// 1. Detect all roots (drives / mount points)
// File.listRoots() works on Windows, macOS, Linux
File[] roots = File.listRoots();
```

*On Windows this returns all letters (`C:\`, `D:\`, …).  
On POSIX systems it normally returns only `/`, but you can easily augment the list by reading `/proc/mounts` or similar.*

---

## 2.  The index data structures

```java
// FileMeta holds the minimal data we need to decide if a file changed
record FileMeta(long size, long lastModified) { }

// The index: absolute path -> FileMeta
class FileIndex {
    private final Map<Path, FileMeta> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    // load from JSON file
    static FileIndex load(Path jsonFile) throws IOException {
        FileIndex index = new FileIndex();
        if (Files.exists(jsonFile)) {
            String content = Files.readString(jsonFile);
            JSONObject obj = new JSONObject(content);
            for (String key : obj.keySet()) {
                JSONObject meta = obj.getJSONObject(key);
                index.map.put(Paths.get(key), new FileMeta(
                        meta.getLong("size"),
                        meta.getLong("lastModified")));
            }
        }
        return index;
    }

    // persist to JSON file
    void save(Path jsonFile) throws IOException {
        JSONObject obj = new JSONObject();
        for (Map.Entry<Path, FileMeta> e : map.entrySet()) {
            JSONObject meta = new JSONObject();
            meta.put("size", e.getValue().size());
            meta.put("lastModified", e.getValue().lastModified());
            obj.put(e.getKey().toString(), meta);
        }
        Files.writeString(jsonFile, obj.toString(2));
    }

    void put(Path p, FileMeta m) {
        map.put(p, m);
    }

    void remove(Path p) {
        map.remove(p);
    }

    // Simple find‑by‑filename (case‑insensitive)
    List<Path> find(String name) {
        return map.keySet()
                .stream()
                .filter(p -> p.getFileName().toString().equalsIgnoreCase(name))
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }
}
```

*You can replace the JSON code with any other format (binary, SQL, etc.).  The `ConcurrentHashMap` gives us thread‑safe updates because the WatchService runs on a different thread.*

---

## 3.  Building the initial index

```java
static void buildInitialIndex(FileIndex index, Path root) throws IOException {
    Files.walkFileTree(root, new SimpleFileVisitor<>() {
        @Override
        public FileVisitResult visitFile(Path file, BasicFileAttributes attrs) {
            index.put(file, new FileMeta(attrs.size(), attrs.lastModifiedTime().toMillis()));
            return FileVisitResult.CONTINUE;
        }
    });
}
```

Call this once per root:

```java
for (File root : roots) {
    buildInitialIndex(index, root.toPath());
}
```

---

## 4.  Persist the index

```java
Path indexFile = Paths.get("file-index.json");
index.save(indexFile);
```

Run this once after the initial scan or after any batch of updates.

---

## 5.  Watch the file system for changes

The Java **WatchService** can only register **individual directories**.  
We therefore walk the tree **once more** – this time registering every directory with the WatchService.

```java
static class FileWatcher implements Runnable {
    private final WatchService watcher;
    private final FileIndex index;

    FileWatcher(FileIndex index) throws IOException {
        this.watcher = FileSystems.getDefault().newWatchService();
        this.index = index;
    }

    // Register a single directory
    void registerDir(Path dir) throws IOException {
        dir.register(watcher,
                StandardWatchEventKinds.ENTRY_CREATE,
                StandardWatchEventKinds.ENTRY_MODIFY,
                StandardWatchEventKinds.ENTRY_DELETE);
    }

    // Walk the tree and register all dirs
    void registerAllRoots(Path root) throws IOException {
        Files.walkFileTree(root, new SimpleFileVisitor<>() {
            @Override
            public FileVisitResult preVisitDirectory(Path dir, BasicFileAttributes attrs) throws IOException {
                registerDir(dir);
                return FileVisitResult.CONTINUE;
            }
        });
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        while (true) {
            WatchKey key;
            try {
                key = watcher.take();          // block until events
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                break;
            }

            Path dir = (Path) key.watchable();

            for (WatchEvent<?> event : key.pollEvents()) {
                WatchEvent.Kind<?> kind = event.kind();

                // Context is the relative path to dir
                Path relPath = (Path) event.context();
                Path fullPath = dir.resolve(relPath);

                try {
                    if (kind == StandardWatchEventKinds.ENTRY_CREATE) {
                        BasicFileAttributes attrs = Files.readAttributes(fullPath, BasicFileAttributes.class);
                        if (attrs.isRegularFile()) {
                            index.put(fullPath, new FileMeta(attrs.size(), attrs.lastModifiedTime().toMillis()));
                        } else if (attrs.isDirectory()) {
                            // new dir → register it (and all its sub‑dirs)
                            registerAllRoots(fullPath);
                        }
                    } else if (kind == StandardWatchEventKinds.ENTRY_MODIFY) {
                        if (Files.isRegularFile(fullPath)) {
                            BasicFileAttributes attrs = Files.readAttributes(fullPath, BasicFileAttributes.class);
                            index.put(fullPath, new FileMeta(attrs.size(), attrs.lastModifiedTime().toMillis()));
                        }
                    } else if (kind == StandardWatchEventKinds.ENTRY_DELETE) {
                        index.remove(fullPath);
                    }
                } catch (IOException ignored) {
                    // In rare cases the file might have vanished already
                }
            }

            boolean valid = key.reset();
            if (!valid) {
                // Directory is inaccessible – stop watching it
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**Start the watcher thread**

```java
FileWatcher watcher = new FileWatcher(index);
for (File root : roots) {
    watcher.registerAllRoots(root.toPath());
}
Thread watchThread = new Thread(watcher, "FileWatcher");
watchThread.setDaemon(true);
watchThread.start();
```

Now the index is kept live.  Every time a file is added, modified, or deleted, the `index` map is updated.  The watcher runs in a background thread so your main UI or service can keep running.

---

## 6.  Using the index for search

```java
public List<Path> findFile(String name) {
    return index.find(name);   // returns empty list if not found
}
```

Because the index lives in memory, a search is **instantaneous**.  If you need persistence across runs, simply load the JSON file on startup (see `FileIndex.load()`).

---

