        if (index.map.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Building initial index (this may take a while)...");
            for (File root : roots) {
                buildInitialIndex(index, root.toPath());
            }
            index.save(jsonFile);
            System.out.println("Initial index saved to " + jsonFile);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Loaded index from " + jsonFile + " (" + index.map.size() + " entries)");
        }

        // Start watcher
        FileWatcher watcher = new FileWatcher(index);
        for (File root : roots) {
            watcher.registerAllRoots(root.toPath());
        }
        new Thread(watcher, "FileWatcher").start();

        // Simple console UI
        BufferedReader console = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));
        while (true) {
            System.out.print("\nEnter file name to search (or 'quit'): ");
            String line = console.readLine();
            if (line == null || line.trim().equalsIgnoreCase("quit")) {
                System.out.println("Exiting...");
                break;
            }
            List<Path> results = index.find(line.trim());
            if (results.isEmpty()) {
                System.out.println("No matches.");
            } else {
                System.out.println("Found " + results.size() + " match(es):");
                results.forEach(System.out::println);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**How to compile/run**

```bash
# Put org.json.java in the same directory (or add a Maven dependency)
javac FileIndexerDemo.java
java FileIndexerDemo
```

---

## 8.  Things to be aware of

| Topic | Notes |
|-------|-------|
| **WatchService limits** | On Windows it can watch many directories (thousands), on Linux you may hit the `watch` limit (`/proc/sys/fs/inotify/max_user_watches`). You can raise it (`sudo sysctl -w fs.inotify.max_user_watches=524288`). |
| **Large file systems** | The initial scan can be slow and memory‑heavy if you keep the whole map in RAM.  You can shard the index, use a database, or write a lazy‑loading layer. |
| **Permissions** | The JVM must have read access to all files you want indexed; otherwise you’ll see `AccessDeniedException`s which the code currently swallows.  You may want to log them. |
| **Network drives / mounts** | WatchService works only on locally mounted file systems; network shares may not fire events.  If you need them you’ll have to resort to periodic rescans. |
| **Persistence format** | JSON is human‑readable but not the fastest.  For production use a binary format (e.g., Kryo, Protobuf) or a small embedded DB (SQLite via JDBC). |
| **Thread safety** | The `ConcurrentHashMap` and the WatchService thread are the only writers.  The main thread can read concurrently without extra locks. |

---

## 9.  Extending the demo

| Feature | How to add it |
|---------|---------------|
| **Full‑text search** | Store file content hashes or use Apache Lucene to index the files. |
| **Duplicate detection** | Add a multimap from hash → list of paths. |
| **GUI front‑end** | Swing, JavaFX or a simple web UI (Spark, Spring Boot). |
| **Cross‑platform mount detection** | On POSIX you can parse `/proc/mounts` or `df -h`. |
| **Incremental persistence** | Write updates to the JSON file after every *n* events or every *t* seconds. |

---

### Bottom line

* **Detect roots** → `File.listRoots()`  
* **Build index** → walk tree, record `size` + `lastModified`  
* **Persist** → JSON (or any format)  
* **Watch** → `WatchService` for create/modify/delete, register every directory  
* **Search** → in‑memory lookup (instant)  

You now have a **fully functional, continuously up‑to‑date file indexer** written in pure Java. Happy indexing!

