Below is a **self‑contained, ready‑to‑run Java example** that:

1. Connects to an IMAP mailbox (supports SSL/TLS).
2. Pulls every message from the *INBOX* (you can change the folder name).
3. Extracts the **subject line** (used as the key) and the **text body** (used as the value).
4. Stores the pair in a `java.util.Hashtable<String, String>`.

> **Important**:  
> • The example uses the **JavaMail** API (now called Jakarta Mail).  
> • Add the following Maven dependencies (or the equivalent JARs) to your project.

```xml
<!-- JavaMail / Jakarta Mail -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.sun.mail</groupId>
    <artifactId>jakarta.mail</artifactId>
    <version>2.0.1</version>
</dependency>
<!-- (Optional) Activation API – usually bundled with Jakarta Mail) -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.sun.activation</groupId>
    <artifactId>jakarta.activation</artifactId>
    <version>2.0.1</version>
</dependency>
```

---

## 1. Java Code

```java
import jakarta.mail.*;
import jakarta.mail.internet.MimeMessage;
import jakarta.mail.internet.MimeMultipart;
import jakarta.mail.search.FlagTerm;

import java.util.Hashtable;
import java.util.Properties;

public class ImapHashtabler {

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1. Configuration – change these to your own values
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    private static final String HOST = "imap.yourmailprovider.com";
    private static final int PORT = 993;                      // IMAPS default
    private static final String USER = "your_email@domain.com";
    private static final String PASSWORD = "your_password";
    private static final String FOLDER_NAME = "INBOX";        // or any other folder

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2. Helper – extracts plain‑text body from a Message
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    private static String getTextFromMessage(Message message) throws Exception {
        if (message.isMimeType("text/plain")) {
            return message.getContent().toString();
        }

        if (message.isMimeType("multipart/*")) {
            MimeMultipart multipart = (MimeMultipart) message.getContent();
            return getTextFromMultipart(multipart);
        }

        // Fallback: try to read as string
        Object content = message.getContent();
        return content != null ? content.toString() : "";
    }

    private static String getTextFromMultipart(MimeMultipart multipart) throws Exception {
        for (int i = 0; i < multipart.getCount(); i++) {
            BodyPart part = multipart.getBodyPart(i);
            if (Part.ATTACHMENT.equalsIgnoreCase(part.getDisposition())) {
                // Skip attachments – adjust if you want to keep them
                continue;
            }
            if (part.isMimeType("text/plain")) {
                return part.getContent().toString();
            } else if (part.isMimeType("text/html")) {
                // Optional: strip HTML tags if you prefer plain text
                return part.getContent().toString();
            } else if (part.getContent() instanceof MimeMultipart) {
                return getTextFromMultipart((MimeMultipart) part.getContent());
            }
        }
        return "";
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3. Main logic – fetch messages and populate Hashtable
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    public static Hashtable<String, String> fetchMessagesIntoHashtable() {
        Hashtable<String, String> table = new Hashtable<>();

        // 3.1 Setup mail session
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("mail.imap.ssl.enable", "true");
        props.put("mail.imap.ssl.trust", HOST);
        props.put("mail.imap.port", String.valueOf(PORT));
        props.put("mail.imap.auth", "true");
        Session session = Session.getInstance(props);

        try {
            // 3.2 Connect to store
            Store store = session.getStore("imap");
            store.connect(HOST, USER, PASSWORD);

            // 3.3 Open folder (INBOX by default)
            Folder inbox = store.getFolder(FOLDER_NAME);
            inbox.open(Folder.READ_ONLY);   // use READ_WRITE if you want to modify flags

            // 3.4 Search for all messages (you can filter)
            Message[] messages = inbox.search(new FlagTerm(new Flags(Flags.Flag.SEEN), false));
            if (messages.length == 0) {
                System.out.println("No new messages found.");
            }

            for (Message msg : messages) {
                String subject = msg.getSubject();
                String body = getTextFromMessage(msg);

                // You can trim, sanitize, or parse the values here
                subject = subject != null ? subject.trim() : "";
                body = body != null ? body.trim() : "";

                // Store into Hashtable
                if (!subject.isEmpty()) {
                    table.put(subject, body);
                    System.out.printf("Stored: [%s] => %s%n", subject, body.substring(0, Math.min(30, body.length())) + "...");
                }
            }

            // 3.5 Clean up
            inbox.close(false);
            store.close();

        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        return table;
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4. Demo / Test
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Hashtable<String, String> mailTable = fetchMessagesIntoHashtable();
        System.out.println("\n=== Hashtable Contents ===");
        for (String key : mailTable.keySet()) {
            System.out.printf("Subject: %s%nMessage: %s%n%n", key, mailTable.get(key));
        }
    }
}
```

### What the code does

| Section | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| **Configuration** | Replace `HOST`, `USER`, `PASSWORD`, etc. with your own IMAP server info. |
| **`getTextFromMessage`** | Recursively pulls the first plain‑text (or HTML) part of the email. |
| **`fetchMessagesIntoHashtable`** | Connects, searches for unseen messages, and puts each `subject → body` pair into a `Hashtable`. |
| **`main`** | Demonstrates usage and prints the table. |

---

## 2. Running the Example

1. **Add Dependencies**  
   If you’re using Maven, copy the dependency snippet above.  
   If you’re not using Maven, download the latest `jakarta.mail.jar` (and `jakarta.activation.jar` if needed) from the Jakarta EE website and add it to your classpath.

2. **Compile**  
   ```bash
