Below is a **complete, self‑contained example** that shows how to upload a file from a web page to a Java servlet.  
It uses the standard Servlet 3.0+ `@MultipartConfig` (no external libraries required) and writes the uploaded file to a directory on the server.

> **What you’ll need**
> * Java 8 or newer
> * A servlet container that supports Servlet 3.0+ (Tomcat 7+, Jetty 9+, WildFly 9+, etc.)
> * A small web.xml (optional if you use annotations only)
> * A folder on the server where the files will be stored (create it beforehand, e.g. `uploaded_files`)

---

## 1.  Project layout (Maven style)

```
src/
 └─ main/
     ├─ java/
     │   └─ com/example/upload/
     │       └─ FileUploadServlet.java
     └─ webapp/
         ├─ WEB-INF/
         │   └─ web.xml   (optional)
         └─ upload.html
```

---

## 2.  The HTML form (upload.html)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>File Upload Demo</title>
</head>
<body>
<h2>Upload a file</h2>
<form action="upload" method="post" enctype="multipart/form-data">
    <input type="file" name="file" required>
    <br><br>
    <button type="submit">Upload</button>
</form>
</body>
</html>
```

> **Tip**: Place `upload.html` in the root of the web‑app (`/webapp` folder).  
> When you deploy, it will be accessible at `http://<host>:<port>/your‑app/upload.html`.

---

## 3.  The Servlet (FileUploadServlet.java)

```java
package com.example.upload;

import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStream;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.StandardCopyOption;
import javax.servlet.ServletException;
import javax.servlet.annotation.MultipartConfig;
import javax.servlet.annotation.WebServlet;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServlet;
import javax.servlet.http.Part;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;

/**
 * A simple Servlet that handles file uploads.
 * It stores the uploaded file in a directory called "uploaded_files"
 * inside the web application root.
 *
 * NOTE: In a production environment you should
 *   • validate the file type and size,
 *   • use a secure location outside the web root,
 *   • generate a unique file name,
 *   • and handle errors gracefully.
 */
@WebServlet("/upload")
@MultipartConfig(
        fileSizeThreshold = 1024 * 1024,      // 1 MB: files larger than this will be written to disk
        maxFileSize      = 10 * 1024 * 1024,  // 10 MB maximum file size
        maxRequestSize   = 12 * 1024 * 1024  // 12 MB total request size
)
public class FileUploadServlet extends HttpServlet {

    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

    // Where uploaded files will be stored relative to the webapp root
    private static final String UPLOAD_DIR = "uploaded_files";

    @Override
    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request,
                         HttpServletResponse response)
            throws ServletException, IOException {

        // Create upload directory if it doesn't exist
        String applicationPath = request.getServletContext().getRealPath("");
        File uploadDir = new File(applicationPath, UPLOAD_DIR);
        if (!uploadDir.exists()) {
            uploadDir.mkdirs();
        }

        // Get the uploaded part (the <input type="file" name="file"> field)
        Part filePart = request.getPart("file"); // throws IOException, ServletException
        if (filePart == null || filePart.getSize() == 0) {
            response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST, "No file uploaded.");
            return;
        }

        // Get the original file name
        String originalFileName = extractFileName(filePart);
        if (originalFileName == null || originalFileName.isEmpty()) {
            response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST, "Invalid file name.");
            return;
        }

        // Optional: sanitize file name, generate unique name, etc.
        File file = new File(uploadDir, originalFileName);

        // Write the file to disk
        try (InputStream input = filePart.getInputStream()) {
            Files.copy(input, file.toPath(), StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            throw new ServletException("File saving failed", e);
        }

        // Respond to the client
        response.setContentType("text/html;charset=UTF-8");
        response.getWriter().println("<p>File uploaded successfully!</p>");
        response.getWriter().println("<p>Saved as: " + file.getAbsolutePath() + "</p>");
    }

    /**
     * Utility method to extract the file name from the HTTP header.
     * Works for most browsers. Not fool‑proof for all clients.
     */
    private String extractFileName(Part part) {
        // Example header: form-data; name="file"; filename="myfile.txt"
        String contentDisp = part.getHeader("content-disposition");
        if (contentDisp == null) {
            return null;
        }

        for (String cd : contentDisp.split(";")) {
            if (cd.trim().startsWith("filename")) {
                String fileName = cd.substring(cd.indexOf('=') + 1).trim().replace("\"", "");
                // In IE, the file path might be sent; strip it
                return fileName.substring(fileName.lastIndexOf(File.separator) + 1);
            }
        }
        return null;
    }
}
```

### Explanation of the key annotations & settings

| Annotation / Setting | Purpose |
|----------------------|---------|
| `@WebServlet("/upload")` | Maps the servlet to the `/upload` URL. |
| `@MultipartConfig` | Enables multipart/form‑data handling. |
| `fileSizeThreshold` | Memory threshold before writing to disk. |
| `maxFileSize` | Maximum size allowed for a single uploaded file. |
| `maxRequestSize` | Maximum size for the whole multipart request. |

---

## 4.  Optional `web.xml` (if you prefer XML configuration)

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<web-app xmlns="http://xmlns.jcp.org/xml/ns/javaee"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://xmlns.jcp.org/xml/ns/javaee
                             http://xmlns.jcp.org/xml/ns/javaee/web-app_4_0.xsd"
         version="4.0">

    <servlet>
        <servlet-name>FileUploadServlet</servlet-name>
        <servlet-class>com.example.upload.FileUploadServlet</servlet-class>
    </servlet>

    <servlet-mapping>
        <servlet-name>FileUploadServlet</servlet-name>
        <url-pattern>/upload</url-pattern>
    </servlet-mapping>
</web-app>
```

> *If you use annotations only, you can omit the `web.xml`.*

---

## 5.  Running the Example

1. **Build & Deploy**  
   * If you’re using a Maven project, simply run `mvn clean package` and deploy the generated WAR to your servlet container.
   * If you’re using an IDE (IntelliJ, Eclipse), just run the project in the embedded Tomcat/Jetty server.

2. **Open the Form**  
   Navigate to: `http://localhost:8080/<your-app-context>/upload.html`

3. **Choose a File & Submit**  
   The file will be stored under `<webapp-root>/uploaded_files/` (you’ll see the path echoed back in the browser).

---

## 6.  Production‑Ready Tips

| Issue | Recommendation |
|-------|----------------|
| **Security** | Never trust the file name. Generate a unique identifier (UUID) or prefix it with a timestamp. Store files outside the web root. |
| **Validation** | Check MIME type & file extension. Reject suspicious content. |
| **Large Files** | Increase `fileSizeThreshold` & `maxFileSize` as needed, but be aware of memory usage. |
| **Error Handling** | Provide user‑friendly error pages. Log exceptions for debugging. |
| **Concurrency** | Use thread‑safe code; the servlet container handles threads automatically. |
| **Clean‑up** | Implement a scheduled task to delete old files if storage is limited. |

---

That’s it!  
You now have a minimal, ready‑to‑run Java servlet that handles file uploads.  
Feel free to adapt it—add progress bars, store metadata in a database, or integrate with cloud storage. Happy coding!

