## TL;DR

> **You can’t build a full‑blown, OS‑wide VPN in “pure C#” without any external components.**  
>   
> *The only thing you can do in pure C# is create a simple *application‑level* tunnel (e.g. a TCP proxy or a user‑mode “VPN” that only forwards the traffic you explicitly open sockets for).  
>   
> To actually make *every* request in the OS go through the VPN you need:  
> 1. A kernel‑mode network driver (e.g. a TAP/TUN device) or a Windows Filtering Platform (WFP) provider.  
> 2. Low‑level APIs that are not exposed by the .NET Framework / .NET Core runtime.  
>  
> Those APIs live in the Windows Driver Kit (WDK) or the native WFP DLLs and cannot be invoked safely from managed code alone without P/Invoke or a C++/CLI wrapper.  
>  
> Bottom line: *without* a driver, *without* the Windows Filtering Platform, *without* a third‑party library, *you can’t intercept system traffic or create a true VPN.*

---

## 1. Why a “pure C#” VPN is impossible

| What a real VPN does | What you can do in C# | Why it fails |
|----------------------|-----------------------|--------------|
| Create a virtual network adapter (TUN/TAP) | Not available in .NET | Only kernel‑mode drivers can expose a virtual NIC. |
| Intercept all packets from the OS | Can’t see raw packets | .NET Sockets only work on a per‑socket basis; you can’t sniff or inject packets. |
| Encrypt/decrypt traffic with TLS/IPSec | You can write TLS code | The *routing* and *packet encapsulation* are missing. |
| Hook into Windows Filtering Platform | Can P/Invoke WFP APIs | Requires native code and a kernel‑mode filter driver. |
| Deliver data to the OS network stack | No, you need a NIC driver | Without a NIC, the OS never knows the data exists. |

So any “VPN” you write entirely in C# will end up being a **proxy** or **tunnelling app** that only forwards traffic for sockets you open in your own process (e.g. a custom browser). It will not affect the traffic of *other* applications unless you also write a driver.

---

## 2. What you *can* do in pure C#

Below is a very simple **TCP tunnel** that forwards data from a local port to a remote port over an encrypted channel (TLS). This is the closest thing you can do in pure C# without drivers or third‑party libraries.

> **Note:** This *does not* make the OS think that your VPN is active. Only traffic that goes through the tunnel (e.g. your own application that explicitly connects to `127.0.0.1:port`) will be forwarded.

### 2.1. Server (VPN Server)

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net;
using System.Net.Sockets;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class TunnelServer
{
    static async Task Main()
    {
        // Load a self‑signed certificate (or use your own)
        var cert = new X509Certificate2("server.pfx", "password");

        TcpListener listener = new TcpListener(IPAddress.Any, 4433);
        listener.Start();
        Console.WriteLine("Server listening on 0.0.0.0:4433");

        while (true)
        {
            var client = await listener.AcceptTcpClientAsync();
            _ = HandleClientAsync(client, cert);
        }
    }

    private static async Task HandleClientAsync(TcpClient client, X509Certificate2 cert)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Client connected from {client.Client.RemoteEndPoint}");
        // Wrap with TLS
        var sslStream = new System.Net.Security.SslStream(client.GetStream(), false);
        await sslStream.AuthenticateAsServerAsync(cert, clientCertificateRequired: false, false);

        // After authentication, the client will send a simple request:
        //   <destIP>:<destPort>\n   followed by the payload
        var reader = new System.IO.StreamReader(sslStream);
        string dest = await reader.ReadLineAsync();
        var parts = dest.Split(':');
        var destIp = IPAddress.Parse(parts[0]);
        var destPort = int.Parse(parts[1]);

        using var remote = new TcpClient();
        await remote.ConnectAsync(destIp, destPort);
        Console.WriteLine($"Connected to {destIp}:{destPort}");

        var remoteStream = remote.GetStream();

        // Bidirectional copy
        var tasks = new[]
        {
            PipeAsync(sslStream, remoteStream),  // Client -> Remote
            PipeAsync(remoteStream, sslStream)   // Remote -> Client
        };
        await Task.WhenAll(tasks);

        client.Close();
        remote.Close();
    }

    private static async Task PipeAsync(System.IO.Stream from, System.IO.Stream to)
    {
        var buffer = new byte[8192];
        int n;
        while ((n = await from.ReadAsync(buffer, 0, buffer.Length)) > 0)
        {
            await to.WriteAsync(buffer, 0, n);
            await to.FlushAsync();
        }
    }
}
```

### 2.2. Client (VPN Client)

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net;
using System.Net.Sockets;
using System.Security.Authentication;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class TunnelClient
{
    static async Task Main()
    {
        // Connect to the server
        var client = new TcpClient("vpn.server.com", 4433);

        // Wrap with TLS
        var sslStream = new System.Net.Security.SslStream(client.GetStream(), false,
            (sender, cert, chain, errors) => true); // Skip cert validation for demo
        await sslStream.AuthenticateAsClientAsync("vpn.server.com",
            clientCertificates: null,
            enabledSslProtocols: SslProtocols.Tls12 | SslProtocols.Tls13,
            checkCertificateRevocation: false);

        var writer = new System.IO.StreamWriter(sslStream) { AutoFlush = true };
        var reader = new System.IO.StreamReader(sslStream);

        // Example: Forward all HTTP traffic from 127.0.0.1:8080 to remote
        var localListener = new TcpListener(IPAddress.Loopback, 8080);
        localListener.Start();
        Console.WriteLine("Listening on 127.0.0.1:8080");

        while (true)
        {
            var local = await localListener.AcceptTcpClientAsync();
            _ = HandleLocalAsync(local, writer, sslStream, reader);
        }
    }

    private static async Task HandleLocalAsync(
        TcpClient local, System.IO.StreamWriter writer, 
        System.IO.Stream sslStream, System.IO.StreamReader reader)
    {
        // Send destination info to server
        // For demo: forward everything to 93.184.216.34:80 (example.com)
        await writer.WriteLineAsync("93.184.216.34:80");

        var remoteStream = sslStream;

        // Copy data between local socket and encrypted tunnel
        var tasks = new[]
        {
            PipeAsync(local.GetStream(), remoteStream),  // Local -> Server
            PipeAsync(remoteStream, local.GetStream())   // Server -> Local
        };
        await Task.WhenAll(tasks);

        local.Close();
    }

    private static async Task PipeAsync(System.IO.Stream from, System.IO.Stream to)
    {
        var buffer = new byte[8192];
        int n;
        while ((n = await from.ReadAsync(buffer, 0, buffer.Length)) > 0)
        {
            await to.WriteAsync(buffer, 0, n);
            await to.FlushAsync();
        }
    }
}
```

#### What this does

1. **Server** listens on port `4433` for TLS connections.  
2. The client establishes a TLS connection, sends a destination in plain text, then forwards all data.  
3. The client also listens locally on `127.0.0.1:8080`. Any local application that connects to this port is transparently tunneled to the remote destination.

#### Limitations

* **Only the traffic you explicitly connect to `127.0.0.1:8080` (or any port you code in the client) will be forwarded.**  
* **No system‑wide routing** – Windows will still use the normal gateway unless you install a TUN/TAP driver and configure the routing table.  
* **No packet filtering or IPsec** – you’re just copying raw bytes over a TLS channel.  
* **No NAT or DHCP** – the remote endpoint must know the client’s IP and routing.  
* **No performance or security guarantees** – this is a toy implementation, not production ready.

---

## 3. The “real” VPN path (requires driver / native code)

If you *really* need a full VPN that intercepts all traffic, you’ll have to:

1. **Write a kernel‑mode driver** (TAP/TUN) or a Windows Filtering Platform (WFP) provider.  
   * The driver exposes a virtual network interface that the OS uses for routing.  
   * You can write that driver in C/C++ (Windows Driver Kit).  
2. **Create a user‑mode service** (your C# code) that talks to the driver via a custom API or the TAP driver’s file descriptor.  
   * The service will encrypt/decrypt packets and send them over a secure tunnel to the server.  
3. **Configure the OS routing table** so that traffic destined for the VPN goes through the virtual NIC.  
   * On Windows this is done with `netsh interface ipv4 add route` or by manipulating the routing table in the driver.

> **Bottom line**: The driver part *cannot* be written in C#; you must use native code (C/C++). Once the driver is in place, you can still keep *most* of your logic in C#, but the driver is indispensable.

---

## 4. Practical recommendation

* **If you only need to test a tunnel or forward traffic from a single application**, the pure C# code above will work.  
* **If you need a true VPN that all applications use**, you will *have to* incorporate a driver:
  * Use the open‑source `TAP-Windows` driver (available on the OpenVPN project).  
  * Write a small C# helper that talks to that driver.  
  * Or, use the Windows Filtering Platform (WFP) via the **Microsoft.Networking.Wfp** package (still a NuGet package, but it’s Microsoft‑provided).  
* **You cannot avoid a third‑party component**. The only “pure” way would be to re‑implement a TAP driver in C++ and expose it to C# via P/Invoke.

---

## 5. TL;DR Summary

| Goal | Feasible in pure C#? | What you really need |
|------|----------------------|----------------------|
| Proxy specific ports | ✔ | No driver needed |
| Intercept all OS traffic | ✘ | Kernel‑mode driver (TAP/TUN) or WFP provider |
| Encrypt and forward packets | ✔ | TLS in user mode (above code) |
| Full VPN experience | ✘ | Driver + routing configuration |

If you are comfortable writing a small driver (or using an existing one), you can keep the bulk of the logic in C#. Otherwise, you’ll have to accept that a “pure C# VPN” is not possible.

