Below is a **self‑contained Java helper** that takes an XML document **as a `String`**, runs an XPath expression against it, and returns the matching XML fragment(s) again as a `String`.

```java
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilder;
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory;
import javax.xml.transform.OutputKeys;
import javax.xml.transform.Transformer;
import javax.xml.transform.TransformerFactory;
import javax.xml.transform.dom.DOMSource;
import javax.xml.transform.stream.StreamResult;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPath;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathConstants;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathFactory;
import java.io.StringReader;
import java.io.StringWriter;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import org.w3c.dom.*;
import org.xml.sax.InputSource;

public class XmlExtractor {

    /**
     * Returns the XML that matches {@code xpathExpr} from the supplied {@code xmlStr}.
     *
     * @param xmlStr      XML document as a string
     * @param xpathExpr   XPath expression – may point to a single node, multiple nodes,
     *                    or a collection of nodes
     * @return String containing the matched XML fragment(s).
     *         If nothing matches an empty string is returned.
     * @throws Exception if parsing / XPath evaluation fails
     */
    public static String extractByXPath(String xmlStr, String xpathExpr) throws Exception {
        // 1. Parse XML string into a DOM Document
        DocumentBuilderFactory dbf = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
        dbf.setNamespaceAware(true);            // Important if you use namespaces
        DocumentBuilder db = dbf.newDocumentBuilder();
        Document doc = db.parse(new InputSource(new StringReader(xmlStr)));

        // 2. Compile and evaluate XPath
        XPathFactory xpf = XPathFactory.newInstance();
        XPath xpath = xpf.newXPath();

        NodeList nodes = (NodeList) xpath.evaluate(xpathExpr, doc, XPathConstants.NODESET);

        // 3. Convert matched nodes back to a string
        List<String> fragments = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < nodes.getLength(); i++) {
            fragments.add(nodeToString(nodes.item(i)));
        }
        // join multiple fragments with newlines (or any separator you prefer)
        return String.join("\n", fragments);
    }

    /**
     * Utility: turn a single DOM node into a pretty‑printed XML string.
     */
    private static String nodeToString(Node node) throws Exception {
        StringWriter sw = new StringWriter();
        TransformerFactory tf = TransformerFactory.newInstance();
        Transformer transformer = tf.newTransformer();

        // optional pretty print
        transformer.setOutputProperty(OutputKeys.OMIT_XML_DECLARATION, "yes");
        transformer.setOutputProperty(OutputKeys.INDENT, "yes");
        transformer.setOutputProperty("{http://xml.apache.org/xslt}indent-amount", "2");

        transformer.transform(new DOMSource(node), new StreamResult(sw));
        return sw.toString().trim();
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /* Example usage – you can remove the main method from your project. */
    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String xml = """
                <?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
                <bookstore>
                  <book genre="fantasy" rating="4.5">
                    <title>Lord of the Rings</title>
                    <author>J. R. R. Tolkien</author>
                    <price>29.99</price>
                  </book>
                  <book genre="sci-fi" rating="4.0">
                    <title>Dune</title>
                    <author>Frank Herbert</author>
                    <price>19.99</price>
                  </book>
                </bookstore>""";

        // Grab the <book> that has a rating >= 4.5
        String xpath = "//book[@rating >= '4.5']";
        String result = extractByXPath(xml, xpath);
        System.out.println("Matched fragment(s):\n" + result);
    }
}
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| 1 | **Parse** the XML string into a `Document`. | Gives us a DOM tree we can walk with XPath. |
| 2 | **Evaluate** the XPath expression. | `NodeList` holds all matching nodes – can be one or many. |
| 3 | **Transform** each matched node back into a string. | Uses `Transformer` which is part of JAXP; produces a well‑formed XML fragment. |

### Things to keep in mind

| Topic | Tips |
|-------|------|
| **Namespaces** | If your XML uses namespaces, set `DocumentBuilderFactory#setNamespaceAware(true)` (already set in the example) and use *prefixed* XPath expressions (e.g., `//ns:book`). |
| **Large Documents** | For very large XML you might want a streaming approach (SAX/StAX). This helper is fine for documents that comfortably fit in memory. |
| **Error handling** | The helper throws `Exception` so you can catch and handle parsing or XPath errors as needed. |
| **Multiple matches** | The code concatenates all matches, separated by a newline. Adjust the separator if you prefer commas, XML envelopes, etc. |

### Customisation

* **Return XML declaration** – change `OutputKeys.OMIT_XML_DECLARATION` to `"no"`.
* **Keep indentation** – set `indent="yes"` and adjust `indent-amount`.
* **Wrap results** – if you need a wrapper element around all matched fragments, add it in the `main` or in the `extractByXPath` method before returning.

With this small helper you can extract any part of an XML document that is represented as a `String`, using the familiar XPath syntax, and get the result back as a normal Java `String`.

