### What’s happening?

The progress‑bar string is built from an **array of emoji characters**.  
When you later call

```js
dotPattern = dotPattern.split('');      // or dotPattern = [dotPattern].join(' ')
```

you’re splitting the string **by UTF‑16 code units** – not by “real” characters.  
Many emojis are *surrogate pairs* (two 16‑bit code units), so the split turns

```
🍔🍕🍟   →  ['🍔', '🍕', '🍟']
```

into something like

```
['\ud83c', '\udf54', '\ud83c', '\udf55', ...]   // the raw UTF‑16 blocks
```

The browser then displays those surrogate blocks instead of the emojis.

The same problem occurs if you use `Array.from([dotPattern])` or the `…` spread operator incorrectly.  
In the snippet you pasted, this line

```js
dotPattern = [dotPattern].join(' ');
```

creates an array with **one element** (the whole string) and joins it with a single space – it *doesn’t split at all*. That’s why you see the “Unicode block” artefacts.

---

## Fix – split by *characters*, not code units

Use `Array.from()` (or the spread operator) **on the string itself**, not on an array that already contains the string.

```js
// ✅ splits into real emoji characters
const chars = Array.from(dotPattern);   // or [...dotPattern]
```

Then you can join them with any number of spaces you want.

---

## Incrementing the “splittage”

If you want to insert *N* spaces between the emojis, just `repeat()` the space string:

```js
const spacing = ' '.repeat(N);          // N = 2, 3, 6, …
dotPattern = Array.from(dotPattern).join(spacing);
```

---

## Full, corrected `handleProgressBar()` function

```js
function handleProgressBar() {
  const progressBarFilled = document.querySelector('.progress-bar-filled');
  const currentTime = Date.now();

  // 1‑second offset so the counter starts at 0
  const secondsPassed = Math.floor((currentTime - overallStartTime) / 1000) - 1;
  if (isGenerating) {
    const activeTime = Math.floor((currentTime - startTime) / 1000);

    // A *short* but varied emoji array – you can use any you like
    const emojiDots = [
      '🍔', '🍕', '🍟', '🍩', '🍦', '🍹',
      '🥗', '🤖', '💻', '🔬', '📡'
    ];

    const numDots     = emojiDots.length;
    const maxDotCount = 3;          // how many “dots” we show at most
    const spacing     = ' '.repeat(2); // <-- change this to 3, 6, … for more/less spacing

    // How many “full” rounds of the array have passed
    const indexOffset = Math.floor(activeTime / (numDots * maxDotCount));

    // The remaining “dot” index for the current cycle
    const remainingTime = activeTime % (numDots * maxDotCount);

    // Build the raw pattern (no spacing yet)
    let dotPattern = '';
    for (let i = 0; i < maxDotCount; i++) {
      const emojiIndex = (remainingTime + i) % numDots;
      dotPattern += (i < remainingTime) ? emojiDots[emojiIndex] : ' ';
    }

    // ✅ split into *characters* and re‑join with the chosen spacing
    dotPattern = Array.from(dotPattern).join(spacing);

    progressBarFilled.innerHTML =
      `processing ${dotPattern} ${activeTime} sec`;
  } else {
    progressBarFilled.innerHTML = `done: ${secondsPassed} sec`;
  }
}
```

### Why this works

| Problem | Why it happens | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| Emojis become blocks | `.split('')` or `[dotPattern].join()` splits by UTF‑16 units. | Use `Array.from(dotPattern)` or `[...dotPattern]`. |
| Wrong number of spaces | You always inserted one space. | `const spacing = ' '.repeat(N)` and then `join(spacing)`. |
| Wrong “splittage” | You were building the string with a fixed loop length. | Compute `maxDotCount` and use a loop that respects that count, then split and re‑join. |

