     font-family: "Segoe UI Emoji", "NotoColorEmoji", "Apple Color Emoji",
                  "Segoe UI", Arial, sans-serif;
   }
   ```

---

### TL;DR

* Emojis look like Unicode blocks because you’re splitting the string by UTF‑16 code units.  
* Use `Array.from(emojiString)` or `[...emojiString]` to get real emoji characters.  
* Join with `'.repeat(N)'` to get any amount of spacing.  
* The fixed code above will show the emojis correctly on any modern browser.

