Below is a **minimal but fully‑functional chat application** that uses:

* **Express** – HTTP server (needed only for serving the static client page).
* **`ws`** – WebSocket library that lets us talk to browsers over `ws://`/`wss://`.
* A tiny client written in plain HTML+JS that connects, sends messages and receives them in real time.

> **Why `ws` instead of Socket.IO?**  
> Socket.IO gives you a lot of features (auto‑reconnect, “rooms”, etc.) out of the box, but it adds a non‑standard protocol on top of WebSockets.  
> If you just need “plain” WebSocket communication, `ws` is lighter and gives you full control over the protocol.

---

## 1. Project structure

```
express-websocket-chat/
│
├─ package.json
├─ server.js
└─ public/
   └─ index.html
```

`public/index.html` is served by Express; it contains the client‑side code.

---

## 2. `package.json`

```json
{
  "name": "express-websocket-chat",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "description": "Simple chat app using Express + ws",
  "main": "server.js",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "node server.js"
  },
  "keywords": ["express", "websocket", "chat"],
  "author": "Your Name",
  "license": "MIT",
  "dependencies": {
    "express": "^4.18.2",
    "ws": "^8.14.2"
  }
}
```

> **Install**  
> ```bash
> npm install
> ```

---

## 3. Server – `server.js`

```js
/**
 *  server.js
 *  -------------------------------
 *  - Creates an Express HTTP server
 *  - Attaches a WebSocket server (`ws`) to it
 *  - Broadcasts every incoming chat message to all connected clients
 *
 *  Run with:   node server.js
 *  Default port: 3000
 */

const http = require('http');
const express = require('express');
const path = require('path');
const WebSocket = require('ws');

// ---------- CONFIG ----------
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

// ---------- EXPRESS ----------
const app = express();

// Serve static files from /public
app.use(express.static(path.join(__dirname, 'public')));

// A simple index route for health‑check (optional)
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.send('WebSocket Chat Server is running. Open http://localhost:' + PORT);
});

// Create HTTP server (needed for WS upgrade)
const server = http.createServer(app);

// ---------- WEBSOCKET ----------
const wss = new WebSocket.Server({ server });

// Keep a set of connected sockets for broadcasting
const clients = new Set();

wss.on('connection', (ws, req) => {
  console.log('New client connected from', req.socket.remoteAddress);
  clients.add(ws);

  // Send a welcome message
  ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'system', text: 'Welcome to the chat!' }));

  // Handle incoming messages
  ws.on('message', (data) => {
    let msg;
    try {
      msg = JSON.parse(data);
    } catch (err) {
      console.warn('Invalid message received:', data);
      return;
    }

    // Expecting: { type: 'chat', user: 'Alice', text: 'Hello' }
    if (msg.type === 'chat' && msg.text && msg.user) {
      const payload = {
        type: 'chat',
        user: msg.user,
        text: msg.text,
        time: new Date().toISOString(),
      };

      // Broadcast to all clients (including sender)
      const payloadStr = JSON.stringify(payload);
      for (const client of clients) {
        if (client.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN) {
          client.send(payloadStr);
        }
      }
    }
  });

  // Handle disconnect
  ws.on('close', () => {
    console.log('Client disconnected');
    clients.delete(ws);
  });

  // Basic error handling
  ws.on('error', (err) => {
    console.error('WebSocket error:', err);
  });
});

// ---------- START ----------
server.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server listening on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
});
```

### How it works

| Event | Action |
|-------|--------|
| `connection` | Adds socket to `clients`, sends welcome |
| `message` | Parses JSON; if a valid chat message, broadcasts to all |
| `close` | Removes socket |
| `error` | Logs error (doesn't crash server) |

> **Note**: No authentication, rooms, or persistence. Just a broadcast chat.

---

## 4. Client – `public/index.html`

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Express WebSocket Chat</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
    #chat { height: 70vh; overflow-y: auto; border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px; }
    #chat .msg { margin-bottom: 8px; }
    #chat .msg .user { font-weight: bold; }
    #input-area { display: flex; padding: 10px; background: #f9f9f9; }
    #input-area input[type="text"] { flex: 1; padding: 8px; font-size: 1rem; }
    #input-area button { margin-left: 5px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>WebSocket Chat</h1>
  <div id="chat"></div>
  <div id="input-area">
    <input id="user" type="text" placeholder="Your name" />
    <input id="msg" type="text" placeholder="Type a message..." />
    <button id="send">Send</button>
  </div>

  <script>
    const chatDiv = document.getElementById('chat');
    const userInput = document.getElementById('user');
    const msgInput = document.getElementById('msg');
    const sendBtn = document.getElementById('send');

    // Connect to the same host (will pick http/https based on page)
    const protocol = location.protocol === 'https:' ? 'wss' : 'ws';
    const socket = new WebSocket(`${protocol}://${location.host}`);

    socket.addEventListener('open', () => {
      console.log('Connected to WebSocket server');
    });

    socket.addEventListener('message', (event) => {
      const data = JSON.parse(event.data);
      if (data.type === 'chat') {
        appendMessage(`${data.user}: ${data.text}`);
      } else if (data.type === 'system') {
        appendMessage(`*${data.text}*`);
      }
    });

    socket.addEventListener('close', () => {
      console.log('Disconnected from server');
      appendMessage('*Disconnected*');
    });

    function appendMessage(txt) {
      const msgEl = document.createElement('div');
      msgEl.classList.add('msg');
      msgEl.textContent = txt;
      chatDiv.appendChild(msgEl);
      chatDiv.scrollTop = chatDiv.scrollHeight;
    }

    sendBtn.addEventListener('click', sendMessage);
    msgInput.addEventListener('keyup', (e) => {
      if (e.key === 'Enter') sendMessage();
    });

    function sendMessage() {
      const user = userInput.value.trim();
      const text = msgInput.value.trim();
      if (!user || !text) return;

      const payload = {
        type: 'chat',
        user,
        text
      };

      socket.send(JSON.stringify(payload));
      msgInput.value = '';
    }
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**What the client does**

1. Connects to the same host that served the page (`ws://…` or `wss://…`).
2. Sends a JSON message on clicking **Send** or pressing **Enter**.
3. Listens for broadcasted messages and appends them to the chat pane.
4. Shows system messages like “Welcome to the chat!”.

---

## 5. Running the app

```bash
# 1. Clone or copy the project
git clone https://github.com/your-username/express-websocket-chat.git
cd express-websocket-chat

# 2. Install dependencies
npm install

# 3. Start the server
npm start
```

Open a browser to <http://localhost:3000> and open multiple tabs/windows. Every message you type will appear instantly in all open instances.

---

## 6. Optional Enhancements

| Feature | How to add |
|---------|------------|
| **Persist messages** | Use a simple in‑memory array or a DB (MongoDB, Redis, SQLite). Broadcast the array on new connections. |
| **User authentication** | Add a login page, store session cookies, send the user ID in the WebSocket handshake. |
| **Rooms / channels** | Keep a map `roomId => Set<ws>`. Send room info with each message (`{ type:'chat', room:'general', ... }`). |
| **Reconnection logic** | On the client, detect `socket.close` and attempt reconnect after a delay. |
| **Security** | Serve over HTTPS and use `wss://` WebSocket. Add rate‑limiting, CORS, and input sanitization. |
| **UI improvements** | Use a front‑end framework (React/Vue) or a library (Bootstrap, Tailwind) for nicer layout. |

Feel free to cherry‑pick the parts you need and build your own full‑featured chat! 🚀

