**What was wrong**

Apple’s Safari/WebKit (used in iOS) does **not support look‑behind** in RegExp.  
In the parser the two helper patterns that strip/keep line‑breaks use look‑behind:

```js
// Patterns.getBreakLineBeforeTag
/(?<=\[\\/?(.*?)\])\n+(?=\[\\/?(?:${tags})\])/

// Patterns.getBreakLineAfterTag
/(?<=\[\\/?(?:${tags})\])\n/
```

When the parser runs on iOS it crashes with *“Invalid regular expression: look‑behind is not supported”*.

**How to fix it**

Replace the look‑behind with a pattern that captures the preceding tag **inside the match** and
decides, in the replacement function, whether to keep or drop the newline.  
The same idea works for the “after” pattern, but we don’t need a look‑ahead there.

```js
// ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  Patterns (look‑behind → look‑ahead + capturing groups)
// ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
class Patterns {
  /* … existing helpers … */

  // 1) newline that comes *after* a tag (any tag) and *before* a tag in tags[]
  //    → we capture the preceding tag and the newline, then test the tag name.
  static getBreakLineBeforeTag(tagTypes, flags = '') {
    const tags = Patterns.prepareTags(...tagTypes); // e.g. 'ul|ol|li'
    //   ([\/?tag])   ← the whole preceding tag (with brackets)
    //   (.*?)        ← the tag name (no brackets)
    //   (\n+)        ← the newline(s)
    //   (?=\[\/?(?:tags)\])  ← ensure the next tag is one of the list
    return new RegExp(
      `(\\[\\/?(.*?)\\])(\\n+)(?=\\[\\/?(?:${tags})\\])`,
      flags
    );
  }

  // 2) newline that follows a *break‑line* tag (no look‑behind needed)
  static getBreakLineAfterTag(tagTypes, flags = '') {
    const tags = Patterns.prepareTags(...tagTypes);
    //   ([\/?tags])   ← the whole tag (with brackets)
    //   (\n+)         ← the newline(s) that follow it
    return new RegExp(`(\\[\\/?(?:${tags})\\])(\\n+)`, flags);
  }
}
```

**Adjust the replacement logic**

`prepareContent` uses the two patterns in two `replace` calls.  
With the new regexes the callback receives three captured groups: the whole
preceding tag (`fullTag`), the inner tag name (`tagName`) and the newline
string (`newlines`).

```js
// ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  BBCodeParser.prepareContent (adjusted callbacks)
// ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
prepareContent(contents = '') {
  if (!contents || typeof contents !== 'string') {
    return '';
  }

  const tags = [...this.tags.getBreakLineTags(), 'li'];

  const beforeTags = Patterns.getBreakLineBeforeTag(['ul', 'ol', 'li'], 'g');
  const afterTags  = Patterns.getBreakLineAfterTag(tags, 'g');

  /* … (other string normalisations) … */

  // keep the newline only when the preceding tag is one of the break‑line tags
  contents = contents.replace(beforeTags, (match, fullTag, tagName, newlines) => {
    if (tags.includes(tagName)) return match;   // keep the newline
    return fullTag;                            // drop the newline
  });

  // simply remove the newline that follows a break‑line tag
  contents = contents.replace(afterTags, '');

  return contents;
}
```

**Result**

* The parser no longer uses any look‑behind regex.  
* The behaviour is identical: newlines between tags are kept or discarded
  according to the original logic.  
* The code runs on iOS (Safari/WebKit) and on every other browser
  without changes.  

You can drop the old `Patterns.getBreakLineBeforeTag` and
`Patterns.getBreakLineAfterTag` definitions and replace them with the
new ones above. All other parts of the parser remain untouched.

