### Short answer  
You can’t “style a plain text node” – the only way to keep the visual
effect that the `<b>`, `<i>` and `<u>` tags give you is to wrap the text in
some element that can carry CSS.  
The usual trick is **replace the tags with `<span>` and copy the style that
the tag would normally give you**.  
After the replacement the original tags are gone, but the text still looks
bold, italic, underlined, etc.

Below is a small, reusable helper that walks an element (or a string that
contains HTML) and replaces every `<b>`, `<i>` and `<u>` with a `<span>`
that carries the corresponding inline style.  It works for nested or
multiple styles as well.

```html
<!-- Example -->
<div id="target">
  <b>Bold <i>and bold‑italic</i> plain</b> <u>underlined</u>
</div>
```

```js
/**
 * Recursively replace <b>, <i>, <u> tags with <span> that has the
 * equivalent inline style.
 *
 * @param {HTMLElement|string} root - The element (or HTML string) to process.
 * @returns {HTMLElement}           - The processed element (with original
 *                                   tags replaced). If a string was passed,
 *                                   it returns a new element with the
 *                                   cleaned HTML.
 */
function stripBoldItalicUnderline(root) {
  const tempContainer = document.createElement('div');

  // Accept either an element or raw HTML string
  if (typeof root === 'string') {
    tempContainer.innerHTML = root;
    root = tempContainer.firstElementChild;
  } else {
    // If we were given an actual element, clone it so we don't mutate the
    // original element until we’re sure we’re happy with the result.
    root = root.cloneNode(true);
  }

  /**
   * Process a node – if it is one of the tags we care about, replace it
   * with a <span> that has the appropriate inline style.  Otherwise
   * walk its children.
   *
   * @param {Node} node
   */
  function process(node) {
    if (node.nodeType !== Node.ELEMENT_NODE) return;

    const tag = node.tagName;

    // Replace only <b>, <i>, <u>
    if (tag === 'B' || tag === 'I' || tag === 'U') {
      const span = document.createElement('span');

      // Add the style that the tag would normally give
      if (tag === 'B') span.style.fontWeight = 'bold';
      if (tag === 'I') span.style.fontStyle = 'italic';
      if (tag === 'U') span.style.textDecoration = 'underline';

      // Move all child nodes into the new span
      while (node.firstChild) {
        span.appendChild(node.firstChild);
      }

      // Replace the old tag with the new span
      node.parentNode.replaceChild(span, node);

      // Now process the span (there may be nested tags inside it)
      process(span);
      return; // the old node has been replaced, so stop here
    }

    // Otherwise recurse into children
    Array.from(node.childNodes).forEach(child => process(child));
  }

  // Start the recursion at the root element
  process(root);

  return root;
}

/* ----------- Usage ----------- */
// If you already have an element on the page:
const processed = stripBoldItalicUnderline(document.getElementById('target'));

// Replace the original element with the cleaned version
document.body.replaceChild(processed, document.getElementById('target'));

// If you have a raw HTML string:
const htmlString = '<b>Hello <i>World</i></b> <u>Again</u>';
const cleanedEl = stripBoldItalicUnderline(htmlString);
document.body.appendChild(cleanedEl);
```

### Why this works

| Original tag | What the browser does | After replacement |
|--------------|-----------------------|-------------------|
| `<b>` | `font-weight: bold` | `<span style="font-weight:bold;">` |
| `<i>` | `font-style: italic` | `<span style="font-style:italic;">` |
| `<u>` | `text-decoration: underline` | `<span style="text-decoration:underline;">` |

Because the span is an inline element, it behaves the same as the original
tag.  The original tags are gone; only the new spans (with inline styles)
are left, so the “tags are hidden” part of your request is satisfied.

### Handling multiple styles

If the text is wrapped in more than one tag at once (e.g. `<b><i>bold‑italic</i></b>`), the recursion
will visit the inner `<i>` first, replace it with a `<span>` that has both
`font-weight: bold` *and* `font-style: italic`, and then the outer
`<b>` will be replaced next, adding `font-weight: bold` again (the second
addition is harmless).  The end result is a single span that carries
both styles.

### Alternatives

* **CSS only** – If you *don’t* need to remove the tags from the DOM,
  you can simply style them with CSS (the browser already does that).
* **Regex** – You could use a regex to replace `<b>...</b>` with
  `<span style="font-weight:bold;">...</span>`, but regexes break down when
  tags are nested or contain other markup.

The DOM‑based solution shown above is robust, respects nesting, and works
in all modern browsers.

