## The idea

1. **Show the image at all times**  
2. **Keep the textual description hidden** until the user hovers over the container (`#ghostinfo`).  
3. When the mouse enters the container the text appears; when it leaves it disappears again.

You can achieve this entirely in **CSS** – no extra JavaScript is required – but if you’d rather have the logic in JS (for example if you want to do something else when the text is shown) a tiny listener works too.

Below are two approaches:

| Approach | What you need to change | Why it works |
|----------|--------------------------|--------------|
| **CSS only** | Wrap the text in an element that can be toggled (`<span class="info">…</span>`) and add a hover rule | CSS handles the show/hide; no script needed |
| **JS + CSS** | Add the same wrapper, then use `mouseenter` / `mouseleave` to toggle the class | Gives you a hook if you later need to trigger something else |

---

## 1. CSS‑only solution

### 1.1  Change the data structure

Your `stationInfo` currently contains the *complete* HTML (image + text).  
Split it into two parts so you can control the text separately.

```js
const stationImages = {
  [strand]:
    '<img src="https://cdn.glitch.global/.../ef1f442c-24bf-40c8-a82b-e89b71c66ecf_3000.jpg?v=1704458402437" onclick="this.requestFullscreen()" class="center" alt="View of the Grand Canal" width="400">',
  [down]:
    '<img src="https://cdn.glitch.global/.../ef390b6c-6021-4738-92fe-2338c38cc2af_3000.jpg?v=1704459300215" onclick="this.requestFullscreen()" class="center" alt="A View of Paris with the Ile de la Cité" width="400">',
};

const stationTexts = {
  [strand]:
    'View of the Grand Canal: Santa Maria della Salute and the Dogana from Campo Santa Maria Zobenigo (c1743) by Bernardo Bellotto. <br><br>From the <a href="https://www.getty.edu/art/collection/object/103RJP#full-artwork-details">Getty\'s Collection Online</a>.',
  [down]:
    'A View of Paris with the Ile de la Cité (c1763) by Jean‑Baptiste Raguenet. <br><br>From the <a href="https://www.getty.edu/art/collection/object/103RBA">Getty\'s Collection Online</a>.',
};
```

### 1.2  Insert image & hidden text

In `generateAndPlay()` (or wherever you set `ghostinfo.innerHTML`) do:

```js
const ghostInfo = document.getElementById("ghostinfo");
ghostInfo.innerHTML =
  stationImages[referencePoint] +          // the image
  '<span class="infoText" style="display:none;">' +   // hidden text
  stationTexts[referencePoint] +
  '</span>';
```

### 1.3  Add the CSS

```css
/* 1.  Keep the text hidden by default  */
#ghostinfo .infoText {
  display: none;          /* or visibility:hidden */
  margin-top: 10px;       /* optional spacing */
}

/* 2.  Show it on hover  */
#ghostinfo:hover .infoText {
  display: block;          /* or visibility:visible */
}
```

That’s it!  
The image is always shown.  
When the user moves the mouse over the whole `#ghostinfo` element the text appears.  
When the mouse leaves it disappears again.

---

## 2. JS + CSS (if you need a callback)

If you ever want to do something else when the text shows up (e.g. log analytics, trigger an animation, etc.) you can toggle a class with a small event handler.

### 2.1  Add the handler

```js
const ghostInfo = document.getElementById("ghostinfo");

ghostInfo.addEventListener("mouseenter", () => {
  ghostInfo.classList.add("text-visible");
});

ghostInfo.addEventListener("mouseleave", () => {
  ghostInfo.classList.remove("text-visible");
});
```

### 2.2  Adjust the CSS

```css
#ghostinfo .infoText {
  display: none;   /* still hidden by default */
}

#ghostinfo.text-visible .infoText {
  display: block;  /* shown when the class is present */
}
```

The HTML and data structure stay the same as in the CSS‑only solution.

---

## 3. Full minimal example

Here’s a tiny, self‑contained snippet that you can drop into your page to test the effect:

```html
