Below is a **starter kit** that shows how the four languages can work together to create a very simple website:

* `index.html` – the public page that loads CSS, JavaScript and the form.
* `style.css` – a tiny stylesheet that gives the page a clean look.
* `script.js` – some client‑side logic that validates the form before it is sent.
* `index.php` – a lightweight form‑processor that receives the POSTed data,
  sanitises it and writes it to a file (you can replace that with a database
  later).  
  It then sends a short “Thank you” page back to the browser.

> **Tip** – This example is intentionally small so you can copy/paste it into a
>  folder, run it on a local PHP server (`php -S localhost:8000`), and start
>  experimenting.  
>  For a production deployment you’ll want to add HTTPS, CSRF protection, a
>  database, and proper error handling – but that’s beyond the scope of this
>  quick‑start.

---

## 1️⃣  Project structure

```
/your‑site/
│
├── index.html        # Public HTML page
├── style.css         # CSS styling
├── script.js         # JavaScript helpers
└── index.php         # PHP form processor
```

> **Why use `index.php` as the processor?**  
>  A common pattern is to have the same filename (`index.php`) handle both the
>  page display (GET request) and the form processing (POST request).  The PHP
>  script checks the request method, so the same URL works for both.

---

## 2️⃣  `index.html`

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>Simple Contact Form</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>
  <main>
    <h1>Get in Touch</h1>
    <form id="contactForm" method="POST" action="index.php" novalidate>
      <label for="name">Name <span class="required">*</span></label>
      <input type="text" id="name" name="name" required>

      <label for="email">Email <span class="required">*</span></label>
      <input type="email" id="email" name="email" required>

      <label for="message">Message</label>
      <textarea id="message" name="message" rows="5"></textarea>

      <button type="submit">Send</button>
    </form>
  </main>

  <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Explanation**

* `novalidate` turns off the browser’s built‑in validation so we can use our own JavaScript check.
* All form fields have `required` attributes except the message, but the JS will enforce the rules.
* The form submits to `index.php` using `POST`.

---

## 3️⃣  `style.css`

