**Short answer**

```html
<textarea id="myTextarea"></textarea>
```

```js
// 1️⃣  If you already have the string with real newlines:
var lines = "test\r\ntest2\r\ntest3";
$("#myTextarea").val(lines);

// 2️⃣  If the string comes from the server with the *escaped* sequence
//     ("test\\r\\ntest2\\r\\ntest3") you need to un‑escape it first:
var raw = "test\\r\\ntest2\\r\\ntest3";
var cleaned = raw.replace(/\\r\\n/g, "\r\n");
$("#myTextarea").val(cleaned);
```

**Why this works**

* `<textarea>` displays the value exactly as it is, preserving line breaks.
* `jQuery.val()` is the correct way to set the value of a `<textarea>` (or any form input).  
  `text()` or `html()` would set the *inner text* of the element, not its value.
* `\r\n` is the standard Windows line‑break sequence. Browsers treat it the same as `\n`, so it shows as a new line.

**A minimal live example**

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>Textarea line breaks</title>
<script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.7.0.min.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
<textarea id="myTextarea" rows="5" cols="30"></textarea>

<script>
$(function () {
    // Example 1 – real newlines in the string
    var text = "test\r\ntest2\r\ntest3";
    $('#myTextarea').val(text);

    // If you prefer to use the escaped version:
    // var escaped = "test\\r\\ntest2\\r\\ntest3";
    // $('#myTextarea').val(escaped.replace(/\\r\\n/g, "\r\n"));
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Common pitfalls**

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| Text appears on one line | The string actually contains the literal characters `\r\n` (backslash‑r, backslash‑n) rather than real newlines. | Un‑escape with `.replace(/\\r\\n/g, "\r\n")` (or `\\n` if you only have `\n`). |
| Text appears with `\r\n` visible | You set the value of a `<div>` or `<p>` instead of a `<textarea>`. | Use `<textarea>` and `.val()`. |
| Text shows as a single line in the textarea but you think it should be multiple | The line breaks were removed earlier (e.g., during server‑side processing). | Verify the server response contains `\r\n` or `\n`. |

**Quick cheat‑sheet for setting a textarea**

```js
// Set value
$('#textareaID').val('line1\r\nline2\r\nline3');

// Get value
var txt = $('#textareaID').val();   // includes real line breaks
```

That’s all you need – just pass the string (with real newline characters) to `val()`, and the browser will render the three lines in the `<textarea>`.

