Yes – but the *native* radio button can’t be styled much.  
To get a fully‑custom look you hide the browser’s default widget and paint your own using a `<label>` (or a wrapper) together with the `::before/::after` pseudo‑elements or a CSS “appearance: none” trick.

Below are a few patterns that work in all modern browsers.  
Feel free to copy‑paste and tweak the colors.

---

## 1. “Appearance = none” (modern browsers)

```html
<style>
  .radio {
    /* hide the default circle */
    -webkit-appearance: none;      /* Safari */
    -moz-appearance: none;         /* Firefox */
    appearance: none;
    width: 20px;
    height: 20px;
    border: 2px solid #555;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: #fff;
    cursor: pointer;
    vertical-align: middle;
    position: relative;
    margin: 0 0.5rem 0 0;
  }

  /* inner dot when checked */
  .radio:checked::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 3px; left: 3px;
    width: 10px; height: 10px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: #ff5722;          /* the color you want */
  }

  /* optional hover/focus styles */
  .radio:hover { border-color: #ff5722; }
  .radio:focus { outline: none; box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(255,87,34,.3); }
</style>

<label>
  <input type="radio" name="fav" class="radio">
  Option A
</label>
<label>
  <input type="radio" name="fav" class="radio">
  Option B
</label>
```

### What this does
* `appearance: none` removes the browser’s native button.
* The outer circle is a plain `border` and `background`.
* The inner dot (`::after`) appears only when the radio is `:checked`.

---

## 2. Using a `<label>` and pseudo‑elements (works everywhere)

```html
<style>
  /* Hide the actual radio */
  .radio { display: none; }

  /* The custom circle */
  .radio + label {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 28px;          /* space for the circle */
    cursor: pointer;
    user-select: none;
  }

  .radio + label::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; top: 0;
    width: 20px; height: 20px;
    border: 2px solid #555;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: #fff;
  }

  /* Inner dot when checked */
  .radio:checked + label::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 5px; top: 5px;
    width: 10px; height: 10px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: #3f51b5;          /* custom colour */
  }

  /* optional hover */
  .radio + label:hover::before { border-color: #3f51b5; }
</style>

<label>
  <input type="radio" name="choice" class="radio" checked>
  Choice 1
</label>
<label>
  <input type="radio" name="choice" class="radio">
  Choice 2
</label>
```

### How it works
* The `<input>` is hidden (`display:none`).
* The `<label>` is the visible circle; its `::before` draws the border, and `::after` draws the dot when the corresponding radio is checked.
* Because the label follows the input, the selector `.radio:checked + label::after` works.

---

## 3. Using a CSS variable to switch colors

```html
<style>
  :root {
    --radio-bg: #fff;
    --radio-border: #444;
    --radio-dot: #4caf50;          /* default checked colour */
  }

  .radio + label::before {
    border: 2px solid var(--radio-border);
    background: var(--radio-bg);
  }
  .radio:checked + label::after {
    background: var(--radio-dot);
  }
</style>
```

Change the colour of all radios simply by redefining the CSS variable:

```css
:root { --radio-dot: #ff5722; }
```

---

## 4. Accessibility considerations

| ✔ | Why it matters |
|---|----------------|
| Use a `<label>` (or `aria-label`) | Click anywhere, not just the tiny radio |
| Keep focus styles (outline / box-shadow) | Keyboard users can see the focused radio |
| Preserve semantics | Screen readers still announce “radio” and its state |

---

## Quick cheat‑sheet

| CSS selector | What it changes |
|--------------|-----------------|
| `appearance: none` | Removes native button |
| `.radio + label::before` | Outer ring / border |
| `.radio:checked + label::after` | Inner dot / fill |
| `:hover`, `:focus` | Hover/focus effects |
| `--radio-dot` (CSS variable) | Easily swap colors |

---

### TL;DR

* The native radio button can’t change its inner dot color directly in CSS.  
* Hide the native input and use CSS to draw a custom circle (with `::before/::after`).  
* Use `appearance: none` if you just want to keep the default shape and only change the dot’s color.

Give one of the snippets above a try and adjust the colors to fit your design!

