## Server‑Sent Events (SSE) – A quick guide + Express.js demo

> **What is SSE?**  
> Server‑Sent Events is a browser‑to‑server communication protocol that lets a server push updates to a client *over a single, long‑lived HTTP connection*.  
> Unlike WebSockets, the traffic is **one‑way**: the server sends data; the client can only read it (it can still POST to the server the usual way).  

| Feature | SSE | WebSocket |
|---------|-----|-----------|
| Direction | **Server → Client** (one‑way) | Bidirectional |
| Transport | HTTP/1.1 persistent connection | TCP/WebSocket handshake |
| Header | `Content-Type: text/event-stream` | N/A |
| Browser API | `EventSource` | `WebSocket` |
| Connection keep‑alive | Automatic `\n\n` messages or comments | Heartbeats (optional) |
| Compatibility | Modern browsers, no polyfill needed | Modern browsers, no polyfill needed |
| Overhead | Low (no framing, plain text) | Higher (binary frames) |
| Use‑case | Push notifications, live feeds, dashboards | Real‑time chat, multiplayer, etc. |

---

## How SSE works

1. **Client opens a connection** – `new EventSource('/events')` (or a plain `GET /events`).
2. Server sets three headers:  
   ```text
   Content-Type: text/event-stream
   Cache-Control: no-cache
   Connection: keep-alive
   ```
3. The server **keeps the connection open** and writes data in the following format:
   ```text
   [event:<name>\n]   // optional event name (default "message")
   data:<payload>\n
   \n
   ```
4. The browser automatically parses the stream, emits a `message` event (or the named event), and passes the payload to the JavaScript handler.

> **A data line may appear multiple times** – the payload is the concatenation of all `data:` lines until the first blank line.

> **Comments** (`:\n`) are ignored by the browser but are a handy keep‑alive mechanism.

---

## Express.js example

Below is a minimal Express app that:

* streams a random number every 2 seconds to **all** connected clients  
* shows how to keep the connection alive  
* demonstrates client‑side consumption

### 1. Server (Node + Express)

```js
// server.js
const express = require('express');
const http = require('http');
const { randomInt } = require('crypto'); // Node 14+

const app = express();
const server = http.createServer(app);

// Store all connected response objects
const clients = new Set();

/**
 * Helper to push an event to a single client
 * @param {http.ServerResponse} res
 * @param {string} eventName  Optional SSE event name
 * @param {any} data          JSON‑serializable payload
 */
function sendEvent(res, eventName, data) {
  if (eventName) res.write(`event: ${eventName}\n`);
  res.write(`data: ${JSON.stringify(data)}\n\n`);
}

/**
 * Keep‑alive heartbeat – browsers may drop a connection if it stays idle
 * for too long.  Every 15 seconds we send a comment line.
 */
function startHeartbeat(res) {
  const interval = setInterval(() => {
    res.write(':\n\n'); // a comment
  }, 15000);

  res.on('close', () => clearInterval(interval));
}

// SSE endpoint
app.get('/events', (req, res) => {
  // 1️⃣ Set the headers – tell the browser it’s an SSE stream
  res.writeHead(200, {
    'Content-Type': 'text/event-stream',
    'Cache-Control': 'no-cache',
    'Connection': 'keep-alive',
    // Optional: prevent proxies from caching
    'X-Accel-Buffering': 'no',
  });

  // 2️⃣ Store the client
  clients.add(res);
  console.log(`Client connected – ${clients.size} total`);

  // 3️⃣ Optional: send a "connected" event
  sendEvent(res, 'connected', { timestamp: Date.now() });

  // 4️⃣ Keep the connection alive
  startHeartbeat(res);

  // 5️⃣ Clean‑up when the client disconnects
  req.on('close', () => {
    clients.delete(res);
    console.log(`Client disconnected – ${clients.size} left`);
  });
});

// Broadcast a random number every 2 seconds
setInterval(() => {
  const payload = { value: randomInt(100), ts: Date.now() };
  console.log('Broadcasting', payload);
  for (const res of clients) {
    sendEvent(res, 'random', payload);
  }
}, 2000);

// Basic route to verify the server is up
app.get('/', (_, res) => res.send('SSE demo – open /events in your browser'));

// Start
const PORT = 3000;
server.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`🚀 Server listening on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
});
```

> **Why a `Set` for clients?**  
> It lets us add/remove quickly, and guarantees uniqueness.

### 2. Client (HTML + JS)

Create a file `public/index.html`:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>SSE Demo</title>
<style>
  body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 2rem; }
  pre { background: #f5f5f5; padding: 1rem; border-radius: .5rem; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<h1>Server‑Sent Events Demo</h1>
<pre id="log">Waiting for events...</pre>

<script>
const logEl = document.getElementById('log');
function log(msg) { logEl.textContent += msg + '\n'; }

// 1️⃣ Create the EventSource
const es = new EventSource('/events');

// 2️⃣ Listen for all events
es.addEventListener('message', e => log('default: ' + e.data));
es.addEventListener('connected', e => log('connected: ' + e.data));
es.addEventListener('random', e => log('random: ' + e.data));

// 3️⃣ Reconnection logic – EventSource does this automatically
es.onerror = e => {
  console.error('SSE error', e);
  log('Error! (will attempt reconnect automatically)');
};

es.onopen = () => log('Connection opened.');
es.onclose = () => log('Connection closed.');
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Serve this static file with Express:

```js
app.use(express.static('public'));
```

Now run `node server.js` and open `http://localhost:3000/`.  
You’ll see a stream of random numbers appearing every 2 seconds.  

---

## Things to keep in mind

| Topic | Note |
|-------|------|
| **CORS** | If your page is hosted on a different origin, set `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or the specific domain). |
| **Compression** | Enabling gzip on the SSE route can break the stream (because it adds a `Content‑Encoding` header). If you need compression, use `express-static-gzip` on the static assets only. |
| **Reconnect time** | The client can specify a custom retry interval using the `retry:` field in the stream (e.g., `retry: 10000\n\n`). |
| **Keep‑alive** | Many browsers close idle connections after ~30 s. Sending a comment line (`:\n`) every 15 s keeps it alive. |
| **Server restarts** | When the server restarts, all connections close automatically. The client will reconnect after the retry interval. |
| **Memory leaks** | Always `delete` the `res` from `clients` on `req.on('close')`. |
| **Event names** | The default event type is `message`. If you use a custom name, handle it with `addEventListener('customName', …)`. |
| **Large payloads** | Each event is a small text chunk. For very large data, consider chunking or using another protocol. |
| **HTTPS** | SSE works over HTTPS just like HTTP. On HTTP, browsers block cross‑origin connections by default. |

---

## Quick “what‑if” comparison

| Scenario | SSE | WebSocket |
|----------|-----|-----------|
| **Simple push notifications** | ✔️ | ✔️ |
| **Chat app** | ❌ (you'd need a second channel for client → server) | ✔️ |
| **Real‑time stock feed** | ✔️ | ✔️ |
| **Binary data** | ❌ (text only) | ✔️ |
| **Low latency** | ✔️ (low overhead) | ✔️ |
| **Browser support** | ✔️ (all modern browsers) | ✔️ (all modern browsers) |

---

## TL;DR

1. **Start an Express route** that sets `Content-Type: text/event-stream`, `Cache-Control: no-cache`, `Connection: keep-alive`.
2. **Keep the response open** and write events using the SSE format (`event:`, `data:`, blank line).
3. **On the client**, create an `EventSource` pointing to that route and listen with `addEventListener`.
4. **Add a heartbeat** (`:\n\n`) to avoid idle timeouts.
5. **Clean up** when `req.on('close')` fires.

That’s it! You now have a live, one‑way channel between your Node/Express server and any browser. Happy streaming!

