```

A `circleMarker` can’t contain text, so the number of carriages can’t be shown “inside” the black dot.  
We’ll replace the `circleMarker` with a **custom div‑icon marker** that is a black circle *and* has a text node inside it.  
When the player buys a carriage we’ll simply update that icon’s HTML so the number changes automatically.

---

## 1. CSS – the black circle with a white number

Add this CSS somewhere in your `<head>` (or a stylesheet):

```css
/* Train marker */
.train-marker {
  position: relative;
  width: 28px;      /* diameter of the circle */
  height: 28px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background-color: black;
  color: white;
  text-align: center;
  line-height: 28px;   /* vertical centre */
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: 12px;
  border: 2px solid white;   /* optional border for contrast */
}
```

You can adjust `width`, `height`, and `font-size` to taste.

---

## 2. JavaScript – create & update the marker

```js
// keep a reference to the train marker
let trainMarker;

// helper that builds the div‑icon with the current number of carriages
function getTrainIcon() {
  return L.divIcon({
    className: "train-marker",
    // you can wrap the number in a <span> if you need further styling
    html: `<span>${numberOfCarriages}</span>`,
    iconSize: [28, 28]   // match the CSS width/height
  });
}

// 1️⃣  When you first start the train, create the marker
function spawnTrain(firstPoint) {
  trainMarker = L.marker(firstPoint, { icon: getTrainIcon() }).addTo(map);
}

// 2️⃣  Every time the number of carriages changes, refresh the icon
function updateTrainIcon() {
  if (trainMarker) {
    trainMarker.setIcon(getTrainIcon());
  }
}
```

