## 3.  Use the sorted array in `createCon`

```js
function createCon() {
  const circle = document.getElementById('circle');
  const len = sortedCon.length;      // use the sorted copy

  for (let i = 0; i < len; i++) {
    const angle = 360 / len;
    const el = document.createElement('span');

    el.innerHTML = `
      <a href="#" style="color:${sortedCon[i].color};">
        ${sortedCon[i].name}<sub>${sortedCon[i].description}</sub>
      </a>
    `;

    // Place each label on the circle
    el.style.transform = `translate(-50%, -50%) rotate(${angle * i}deg) translateY(-50%)`;
    el.style.top = '50%';
    el.style.left = '50%';

    // **Optional:** set z‑index based on the sorted position
    // Earlier items (lower timeline) will get lower z‑index → appear behind
    el.style.zIndex = i;              // or `len - i` if you want reverse

    circle.appendChild(el);
  }
}
```

> **Why `z-index`?**  
> All the `<span>`s are absolutely positioned on top of the circle.  
> The stacking order is determined by `z-index`. By assigning it from `0` to `len‑1` (or the reverse) we can force the *earlier* gods to sit under the *later* ones, creating a visual timeline.

