Below is a **complete, production‑ready Mongoose schema layer** you can drop into a Node‑JS project that powers a chat application.  
It covers:

| Concept | What it represents | Why it matters |
|---------|--------------------|----------------|
| `User`  | Registered people who can chat | Auth, profile, typing status |
| `ChatRoom` | A conversation (private 1‑to‑1 or group) | Routing, permissions |
| `Message` | The actual payload exchanged | History, read receipts |
| `ChatMember` | Membership & per‑user metadata (lastSeen, role, muted) | Per‑user settings |

You can swap the “chat room” for a more generic “conversation” or “thread” name – the structure stays the same.

---

## 1️⃣ Install

```bash
npm install mongoose
# (or yarn add mongoose)
```

## 2️⃣ Mongoose connection (quick boot‑strap)

```js
// db.js
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
const uri = process.env.MONGO_URI || 'mongodb://localhost:27017/chat_app';

module.exports = async function connect() {
  await mongoose.connect(uri, {
    useNewUrlParser: true,
    useUnifiedTopology: true,
  });
  console.log('MongoDB connected');
};
```

---

## 3️⃣ Schemas

### 3.1 User

```js
// models/User.js
const { Schema, model } = require('mongoose');

const userSchema = new Schema(
  {
    username: { type: String, required: true, unique: true, trim: true },
    email:    { type: String, required: true, unique: true, lowercase: true, trim: true },
    passwordHash: { type: String, required: true },

    // Optional profile fields
    avatarUrl: String,
    bio: String,
    status: { type: String, enum: ['online', 'offline', 'away', 'do_not_disturb'], default: 'offline' },

    // For quick typing status
    lastSeen: { type: Date, default: Date.now },

    // Fallback to keep an array of active chat ids
    activeChats: [{ type: Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: 'ChatRoom' }],
  },
  { timestamps: true } // createdAt, updatedAt
);

// Indexes for performance
userSchema.index({ username: 1 });
userSchema.index({ email: 1 });

module.exports = model('User', userSchema);
```

> **Tip** – Keep `passwordHash` out of `toJSON()` and `toObject()`:

```js
userSchema.set('toJSON', { transform(doc, ret) {
  delete ret.passwordHash;
  return ret;
}});
```

---

### 3.2 ChatRoom

```js
// models/ChatRoom.js
const { Schema, model } = require('mongoose');

const chatRoomSchema = new Schema(
  {
    // Human readable name (group name or other user’s name)
    name: { type: String, required: true, trim: true },

    // One‑to‑one or multi‑user
    isGroup: { type: Boolean, default: false },

    // List of members (user references + role / settings)
    members: [
      {
        user: { type: Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: 'User', required: true },
        role: { type: String, enum: ['owner', 'admin', 'member'], default: 'member' },
        mutedUntil: Date, // if muted for a time period
        lastSeen: Date,   // per‑user last seen timestamp
      },
    ],

    // Owner for 1‑to‑1 chats you can treat as a special `admin`
    owner: { type: Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: 'User', required: true },

    // Optional group image
    imageUrl: String,

    // Flags
    archived: { type: Boolean, default: false },

    // Metadata
    description: String,
  },
  { timestamps: true }
);

// For quick lookup: who’s a member of which chats
chatRoomSchema.index({ 'members.user': 1, isGroup: 1 });

// If you want to enforce uniqueness of 1‑to‑1 chat between 2 users
chatRoomSchema.index(
  { 'members.user': 1, isGroup: 0 },
  { unique: true, partialFilterExpression: { isGroup: false } }
);

module.exports = model('ChatRoom', chatRoomSchema);
```

---

### 3.3 Message

```js
// models/Message.js
const { Schema, model } = require('mongoose');

const messageSchema = new Schema(
  {
    chatRoom: { type: Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: 'ChatRoom', required: true, index: true },

    sender:   { type: Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: 'User', required: true, index: true },

    // Text, image, video, etc. – using a flexible `content` object
    content: {
      type: Map, // key: string → value: mixed (string, URL, etc.)
      default: {},
    },

    type: { type: String, enum: ['text', 'image', 'video', 'file', 'sticker'], default: 'text' },

    // Flags
    edited: { type: Boolean, default: false },
    deleted: { type: Boolean, default: false },

    // Read receipts – per user
    readBy: [{ type: Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: 'User' }],

    // For edit history
    editHistory: [
      {
        content: {
          type: Map,
          default: {},
        },
        editedAt: { type: Date, default: Date.now },
      },
    ],
  },
  { timestamps: true }
);

// Fast query by chatRoom and createdAt
messageSchema.index({ chatRoom: 1, createdAt: -1 });

module.exports = model('Message', messageSchema);
```

> **Why a Map?**  
> It lets you store arbitrary key/value pairs (e.g. `text`, `caption`, `fileUrl`). If you prefer strictness you can replace it with a schema that matches your content model.

---

### 3.4 ChatMember (Optional, if you need a dedicated collection)

If you want a separate collection to keep per‑user room metadata (lastSeen, notifications, etc.), create a separate schema. Otherwise, the `members` array in `ChatRoom` is sufficient.

```js
// models/ChatMember.js (optional)
const { Schema, model } = require('mongoose');

const chatMemberSchema = new Schema(
  {
    chatRoom: { type: Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: 'ChatRoom', required: true },
    user:     { type: Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: 'User', required: true },

    role:      { type: String, enum: ['owner', 'admin', 'member'], default: 'member' },
    mutedUntil: Date,
    lastSeen: Date,
    notificationsEnabled: { type: Boolean, default: true },
  },
  { timestamps: true }
);

chatMemberSchema.index({ chatRoom: 1, user: 1 }, { unique: true });

module.exports = model('ChatMember', chatMemberSchema);
```

---

## 4️⃣ Using the schemas

```js
// chatService.js
const User = require('./models/User');
const ChatRoom = require('./models/ChatRoom');
const Message = require('./models/Message');

// Create a 1‑to‑1 chat
async function createOneToOneChat(userIdA, userIdB) {
  // Check if a chat already exists
  let chat = await ChatRoom.findOne({
    isGroup: false,
    members: { $all: [userIdA, userIdB], $size: 2 },
  });

  if (!chat) {
    chat = await ChatRoom.create({
      name: `Chat ${userIdA}-${userIdB}`,
      isGroup: false,
      owner: userIdA,
      members: [
        { user: userIdA, role: 'member' },
        { user: userIdB, role: 'member' },
      ],
    });
  }

  return chat;
}

// Send a message
async function sendMessage(chatId, senderId, content, type = 'text') {
  const message = await Message.create({
    chatRoom: chatId,
    sender: senderId,
    content,
    type,
    readBy: [senderId], // Sender has read it
  });

  // Optionally, emit via Socket.io here

  return message;
}

// Mark a message as read
async function markRead(chatId, messageId, readerId) {
  await Message.updateOne(
    { _id: messageId, chatRoom: chatId, readBy: { $ne: readerId } },
    { $push: { readBy: readerId } }
  );
}

// Get last 50 messages with pagination
async function fetchMessages(chatId, limit = 50, beforeMsgId = null) {
  const query = { chatRoom: chatId };
  if (beforeMsgId) {
    const beforeMsg = await Message.findById(beforeMsgId);
    if (beforeMsg) query.createdAt = { $lt: beforeMsg.createdAt };
  }

  return Message.find(query)
    .sort({ createdAt: -1 })
    .limit(limit)
    .populate('sender', 'username avatarUrl');
}
```

---

## 5️⃣ Indexes & Performance

| Index | Why |
|-------|-----|
| `User.username + User.email` | Fast lookup for login & uniqueness. |
| `ChatRoom.members.user + ChatRoom.isGroup` | Quickly find all chats a user belongs to. |
| `Message.chatRoom + Message.createdAt` | Fast message history queries. |
| `Message.chatRoom + Message.deleted` | If you want to hide deleted messages by default. |
| `Message.readBy` | Counting read receipts can be optimized. |

**Tip:**  
- **TTL** for temporary data (e.g., typing indicators) – you can store a `TypingIndicator` collection with an `expiresAt` field and set a TTL index:  
  ```js
  typingSchema.index({ expiresAt: 1 }, { expireAfterSeconds: 0 });
  ```
- Use **sharding** if you anticipate millions of messages per chat.  
  For a typical SaaS chat app, a single instance with proper indexes will handle ~10k–20k messages per second comfortably.

---

## 6️⃣ Security & Validation

- Never expose `passwordHash`. Use Mongoose `select: false` or custom `toJSON` transformation.  
- Validate `email` and `username` formats using a validator library (`validator` npm package).  
- For message content, use a whitelist of allowed fields to guard against injection.  
- Rate‑limit message posting per user per second (e.g., `express-rate-limit` or Redis‑based solution).  
- When storing files (images, videos), keep the URL only in the database; keep the actual files on S3 / Cloudflare R2 or similar.  

---

## 7️⃣ Full Project Layout (sample)

```
/project-root
├─ src
│  ├─ db.js
│  ├─ models
│  │   ├─ User.js
│  │   ├─ ChatRoom.js
│  │   ├─ Message.js
│  │   └─ ChatMember.js (optional)
│  ├─ services
│  │   └─ chatService.js
