  When the user clicks anywhere in the page, the tree’s `clicked` class is added (and the inner‑HTML is refreshed so the animation restarts).
* We keep the tree’s own `.click()` handler out of the picture – otherwise the document click would fire twice when you click the tree.

```js
/* -------------  Christmas‑Tree Animation ------------- */
$(function () {
  /* 1️⃣  Reset the animation when the mouse leaves the tree  */
  $('#christmas-tree').mouseleave(function () {
    $(this).removeClass('clicked');
  });

  /* 2️⃣  Trigger the animation *only* on a document click   */
  $(document).on('click', function () {
    // Grab the tree, add the “clicked” class (which starts the animation)
    // and refresh its HTML to reset the animation sequence.
    $('#christmas-tree')
      .addClass('clicked')
      .html($('#christmas-tree').html());
  });
});
```

---

### Why this works

| What you wanted | What we’re doing | Why it matters |
|-----------------|------------------|----------------|
| “only triggered when the document is clicked on” | `$(document).on('click', …)` | The click handler lives on `document`, not on `#christmas-tree`. |
| Keep the leave‑reset logic | `mouseleave` stays on `#christmas-tree` | The animation still stops when you move the mouse away. |
| Avoid duplicate triggers | No `.click()` on `#christmas-tree` | Prevents a second fire when you click the tree itself. |

