Below is a **complete, self‑contained example** that shows how to

1. Take some text from the user via an HTML form.
2. Create a new text file on the server (or just send it to the browser for download).
3. Handle all the small details (file‑name safety, permissions, etc.) so you don’t run into common pitfalls.

---

## 1. Quick‑Start – “Just download a text file”

If you don’t need to keep the file on the server, the easiest way is to let PHP send it straight to the browser with the proper headers.

```php
<?php
// download.php
if (isset($_POST['content'])) {
    $text = $_POST['content'];

    // Tell the browser we’re sending a text file
    header('Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8');
    header('Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="myfile.txt"');
    header('Content-Length: ' . strlen($text));

    // Output the content and finish
    echo $text;
    exit;
}
?>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Send Text as Download</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Write some text and hit Download</h1>
    <form method="post">
        <textarea name="content" rows="10" cols="60" placeholder="Enter text here…"></textarea><br>
        <button type="submit">Download as Text File</button>
    </form>
</body>
</html>
```

> **How it works**  
> 1. The form posts back to the same page.  
> 2. When `$_POST['content']` is set, PHP sends three headers:
>    * `Content-Type` – tells the browser we’re sending plain text.  
>    * `Content-Disposition: attachment;` – forces a “Save As” dialog.  
>    * `Content-Length` – optional but helps the browser know how big the file is.  
> 3. `echo $text;` writes the user’s input straight to the HTTP body.  
> 4. `exit;` stops any further output.

---

## 2. “Save the file on the server and then offer a link”

Sometimes you want to keep the file (e.g., for logging, later download, etc.).  
In that case you write it to a directory and then give the user a link.

```php
<?php
// save_and_link.php
$targetDir = __DIR__ . '/uploads';   // make sure this folder exists

if (!is_dir($targetDir)) {
    mkdir($targetDir, 0755, true);
}

if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'POST' && isset($_POST['content'])) {
    $content = $_POST['content'];

    // Build a safe file name
    $baseName = 'document_' . date('Ymd_His') . '_' . uniqid();
    $fileName = $baseName . '.txt';
    $filePath = $targetDir . '/' . $fileName;

    // Write the file
    if (file_put_contents($filePath, $content) !== false) {
        // Make sure the file is readable by the webserver
        chmod($filePath, 0644);

        // Show a download link (or redirect to the file)
        $fileUrl = 'uploads/' . $fileName;
        echo "<p>File created successfully! <a href=\"$fileUrl\" download>Click here to download.</a></p>";
    } else {
        echo '<p style="color:red;">Error: Could not write the file.</p>';
    }
}
?>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Save Text to Server</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Enter Text → Save &amp; Link</h1>
    <form method="post">
        <textarea name="content" rows="10" cols="60" placeholder="Enter text here…"></textarea><br>
        <button type="submit">Save File</button>
    </form>
</body>
</html>
```

> **Key Points**  
> * **Directory permissions** – The `uploads/` folder must be writable by the web server user (e.g., `www-data` on Ubuntu).  
> * **File‑name safety** – We create a unique file name (`uniqid()`) and do **not** accept user‑supplied file names to avoid directory traversal.  
> * **chmod** – After writing we set the file to `0644` (owner read/write, everyone else read) so it can be served.  
> * **Serving the file** – By linking to `uploads/filename.txt` the web server will send the file as a static resource.  
>   If you want to force a download you can add `?download=1` to the URL and handle it in PHP, or simply add the `download` attribute on the `<a>` tag as shown.

---

## 3. A Few Things to Keep in Mind

| Topic | Why it matters | What to do |
|-------|----------------|------------|
| **File‑system permissions** | PHP can’t write if the directory isn’t writable. | `chmod 0755` for the folder, `chmod 0644` for files; or set the correct owner. |
| **Input validation** | Avoid XSS or accidental injection of large data. | Trim input, limit size (`strlen($content) <= 100000` or use `max_length` on textarea). |
| **Path traversal** | Users could try `../../etc/passwd` if you accept file names. | Never use raw input in paths; always generate or sanitize names. |
| **Large files** | Memory limits may bite. | Use `fopen()/fwrite()` streams or `file_put_contents()` with `FILE_APPEND` if you’re appending. |
| **Encoding** | Mismatched encodings lead to garbled text. | Explicitly set `UTF-8` in headers and handle input accordingly. |
| **Security** | Storing files in public directories may expose sensitive data. | Store in protected areas, use authentication to serve them, or delete after download. |

---

### TL;DR

1. **Simple download** – just send headers and `echo $text`.  
2. **Save to disk** – `file_put_contents()` (or `fopen/fwrite`) to a writable folder, then link or redirect to it.  
3. **Always** validate, sanitize, and give the folder the right permissions.

Feel free to copy the snippets into your own project and tweak the file names, locations, or form fields as you like!

