Below is a **complete, self‑contained example** that shows how to create a tiny PHP web page that:

1. Lets a visitor upload *any* video file (MP4, AVI, MKV, etc.).
2. Uses **FFmpeg** (command‑line) to transcode the uploaded file to a clean MP4 (H.264‑video + AAC‑audio).
3. Sends the newly‑created MP4 back to the browser and immediately starts the download.

> ⚠️ **Security note** – This is a minimal example meant for learning or a very small test site.  
> If you deploy it for real use you must:
> * Validate file size & type aggressively.
> * Use a safe “uploads” directory that is **not** web‑accessible.
> * Protect against directory traversal & other injection attacks.
> * Consider queueing / background processing for large files.

---

## 1. Prerequisites

| Item | What you need | How to install |
|------|---------------|----------------|
| PHP (>= 7.0) | Web server + PHP | Usually installed with your OS or XAMPP/LAMP |
| FFmpeg | Command‑line video converter | ```sudo apt-get install ffmpeg``` (Ubuntu) <br>```brew install ffmpeg``` (macOS) <br>or download the Windows binaries from <https://ffmpeg.org/download.html> |
| File‑system write permissions | PHP must be able to write into the `tmp/` directory | `chmod 755 tmp` (or 775/777 as needed) |

> **Tip** – Verify FFmpeg is on the server by running `ffmpeg -version` from the command line where PHP will execute.

---

## 2. Project layout

```
/your-site/
├─ index.php        ← form to upload
├─ convert.php      ← handles upload, conversion, and download
└─ tmp/            ← temporary storage (should not be publicly accessible)
```

Make sure the `tmp/` directory exists and is writable by the web server user.

---

## 3. `index.php` – the upload form

```php
<?php
// index.php
?>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Video ➜ MP4 Converter</title>
<style>
body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; margin: 2rem; }
form { border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 1rem; width: 400px; }
input[type=file] { width: 100%; }
input[type=submit] { margin-top: 0.5rem; }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Upload a Video</h1>
<p>Select a video file (MP4, AVI, MKV, …) and press **Convert**.</p>

<form action="convert.php" method="post" enctype="multipart/form-data">
    <label for="video_file">Choose video file:</label>
    <input type="file" name="video_file" id="video_file" required accept="video/*">
    <input type="submit" value="Convert">
</form>

</body>
</html>
```

---

## 4. `convert.php` – upload → convert → download

```php
<?php
// convert.php
ini_set('max_execution_time', 0);     // allow long runs
set_time_limit(0);                    // just in case

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Basic checks
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
if (!isset($_FILES['video_file']) || $_FILES['video_file']['error'] !== UPLOAD_ERR_OK) {
    die('⚠️  No file uploaded or upload error.');
}

$uploadedFile = $_FILES['video_file'];
$originalName = basename($uploadedFile['name']);

// Optional: restrict to a maximum size (e.g. 100 MiB)
// $maxSize = 100 * 1024 * 1024; // 100 MiB
// if ($uploadedFile['size'] > $maxSize) {
//     die('⚠️  File is too large.');
// }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Save the upload to a temporary location
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
$uploadTmp = __DIR__ . '/tmp/';
$randomId = bin2hex(random_bytes(8));
$originalPath = $uploadTmp . $randomId . '_' . $originalName;

// Move uploaded file
if (!move_uploaded_file($uploadedFile['tmp_name'], $originalPath)) {
    die('⚠️  Failed to move uploaded file.');
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Convert to MP4 using ffmpeg
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
$mp4Name   = $randomId . '_' . pathinfo($originalName, PATHINFO_FILENAME) . '.mp4';
$mp4Path   = $uploadTmp . $mp4Name;

// Build ffmpeg command – feel free to tweak encoding parameters
$ffmpeg = 'ffmpeg'; // if ffmpeg is in PATH. otherwise give full path
$cmd = sprintf(
    '%s -y -i %s -c:v libx264 -preset fast -crf 23 -c:a aac -b:a 128k %s 2>&1',
    escapeshellcmd($ffmpeg),
    escapeshellarg($originalPath),
    escapeshellarg($mp4Path)
);

exec($cmd, $output, $returnVar);

if ($returnVar !== 0) {
    // ffmpeg failed – show the error log
    echo '<pre>' . htmlentities(implode("\n", $output)) . '</pre>';
    unlink($originalPath);   // clean up
    die('⚠️  FFmpeg conversion failed.');
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣  Send the MP4 back to the client
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
header('Content-Description: File Transfer');
header('Content-Type: video/mp4');
header('Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="' . $mp4Name . '"');
header('Content-Transfer-Encoding: binary');
header('Expires: 0');
header('Cache-Control: must-revalidate');
header('Pragma: public');
header('Content-Length: ' . filesize($mp4Path));

// Clear output buffering to avoid memory issues
while (ob_get_level()) {
    ob_end_clean();
}

readfile($mp4Path);

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5️⃣  Clean up temporary files
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
unlink($originalPath);
unlink($mp4Path);

exit;
```

### How it works

1. **Upload handling** – `$_FILES` stores the uploaded file in a temporary location.  
   We `move_uploaded_file()` to our own `tmp/` folder.

2. **FFmpeg conversion** –  
   ```bash
   ffmpeg -y -i input.avi -c:v libx264 -preset fast -crf 23 -c:a aac -b:a 128k output.mp4
   ```
   * `-y` automatically overwrites output files.  
   * `-preset fast` balances speed/quality.  
   * `-crf 23` is a good default quality for H.264.  
   * `-c:a aac -b:a 128k` gives a decent audio bitrate.

   The command is wrapped in `exec()` with proper escaping to avoid shell injection.

3. **Download** – The server sends the MP4 with `Content-Disposition: attachment` so browsers pop the *Save As* dialog automatically.

4. **Cleanup** – The temporary files are deleted immediately after the download is sent.

---

## 5. Testing

1. Upload a video file via the form on `index.php`.  
2. If FFmpeg finishes successfully, the browser should prompt to download an MP4 named after the original file (but with `.mp4`).  
3. The server logs (or the browser console) should show the conversion output only if an error occurs.

---

## 6. Things you may want to tweak

| Feature | What you might change | How |
|---------|-----------------------|-----|
| **Supported input extensions** | Only allow certain extensions | Add `in_array()` check on `$uploadedFile['type']` or use a MIME‑type library |
| **Maximum file size** | Prevent huge uploads | `$maxSize = 500 * 1024 * 1024; // 500 MiB` |
| **Concurrent uploads** | Prevent race conditions | Use PHP sessions or a queue; or let FFmpeg write to a unique filename (already done) |
| **Better encoding** | Use a higher‑quality preset or variable bitrate | Change `-preset` or add `-crf` or `-maxrate` parameters |
| **HTTPS** | Encrypt traffic | Configure your web server with an SSL cert (Let's Encrypt) |
| **Progress bar** | Show conversion progress | Use long‑polling, WebSockets, or `ffprobe` to read the log. |

---

## 7. Security Checklist

| Check | Why | How |
|-------|-----|-----|
| **Upload directory** | Prevent direct web access to uploaded files | Place `tmp/` outside the document root or use `.htaccess deny from all` |
| **File validation** | Stop malicious scripts disguised as videos | Validate MIME type & extension; optionally use `ffprobe` to inspect the file |
| **Permissions** | Limit who can read/write | `chmod 0750 tmp` + owner = web server user |
| **FFmpeg path** | Avoid shell injection | Use the full absolute path to `ffmpeg` or `escapeshellarg()` as shown |
| **Error handling** | Don't leak internal paths | Hide full ffmpeg logs to users; log them to a secure file instead |

---

### Wrap‑up

You now have a **complete, runnable** PHP example that:

* Uploads any video.
* Transcodes it to MP4 with FFmpeg.
* Forces an instant download.
* Cleans up after itself.

Just drop the two files in a PHP‑capable web server, ensure `tmp/` exists, and install FFmpeg. 🎬 Happy coding!

